/* THE AOT RUNTIME ABI: every helper compiled JavaScript may call, declared once.
 *
 * CT_AOT_HELPER(name, return_type, (parameters), may_throw, may_reenter,
 *               is_safepoint)
 * CT_AOT_COVERS(helper, opcode)          one line per opcode a helper serves
 *
 * PRINCIPLE 11: THE RUNTIME OWNS THIS FILE AND THE COMPILER READS IT. ctbrowser
 * expands it to declare and define the helpers; ctcompile includes the SAME
 * file and expands it to build its descriptor table and the declarations it
 * emits. There is no generator and no dependency - an X-macro rather than
 * TableGen, because generating this into ctbrowser would make a runtime-only
 * build require llvm-tblgen and break Principles 2 and 8.
 *
 * THE POINT IS THAT DRIFT IS A COMPILE ERROR. A renamed, removed or misspelled
 * helper fails to build in ctcompile rather than producing a link error, a
 * crash, or - worst - a silently wrong call.
 *
 * PRINCIPLE 3 IS WHY THE LIST IS SHORT: AOT is a BACKEND of this runtime, not a
 * second JavaScript engine. Every row below delegates to semantics the
 * interpreter already implements. Nothing here reimplements property lookup,
 * conversion, calls, classes, promises, proxies, iterators or modules.
 *
 * THE FLAGS ARE OBLIGATIONS, NOT DESCRIPTIONS:
 *   may_throw     the caller must test the status it returns
 *   may_reenter   this helper can run user JavaScript before it returns
 *   is_safepoint  a collection can happen inside it, so every live value the
 *                 caller holds must be reachable from a root ACROSS the call.
 *                 The collector is precise and walks exactly the roots in
 *                 ctcompile/include/ctcompile/JavaScript/GCRoots.def; a value
 *                 living only in a native frame is reachable from NONE of them.
 *
 * TWO TIERS OF FAILURE, and they are not the same thing. raise() sets a flag no
 * try/catch can see - the allocation ceiling, a missing module - and the only
 * correct response is to unwind the whole native chain. thrown_ plus
 * unwind_to_handler is the catchable path, and it completes INSIDE the callee:
 * by the time a helper returns there is no exception in flight, only a frame
 * stack that is shorter than it was. That is why the status vocabulary has no
 * value meaning "threw".
 *
 * COVERAGE: 83 of the 93 opcodes in script/bytecode_opcodes.def are served here.
 * The other ten need no runtime call and are listed at the bottom of this file
 * with the reason. Nothing is uncovered by accident.
 *
 * THE POINT OF ALL THIS IS SPEED, SO READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A ROW.
 *
 * A CALL PER OPCODE WOULD BE AN INTERPRETER WITHOUT THE DISPATCH LOOP - and a
 * slower one, because it would pay a call, a spill and a reload where the run
 * loop pays a jump. This table is a SEMANTIC CONTRACT, not an instruction
 * schedule, and it earns its keep in two different ways on the two backends:
 *
 *   EmitC (Phase 10A, THE PRIMARY BACKEND) emits ordinary C++ that INCLUDES the
 *   runtime headers, so a call to a helper is a call to an inline function the
 *   host compiler sees the body of. At -O2 the fast path - a number that is
 *   already a number, a property that is a plain data slot - inlines away and
 *   the slow path stays a call. That is why EmitC went first: the LLVM path
 *   needs a bitcode archive and LTO to get the same thing.
 *
 *   The LLVM dialect backend (Phases 11-12A) cannot inline across a frozen C
 *   ABI, so for THAT backend these signatures are the boundary and the fast
 *   paths have to be open-coded in the IR before the call is emitted.
 *
 * Which gives the rule for every row here: A HELPER IS THE SLOW PATH. Where a
 * check is a tag test against value's constants, a backend must be free to
 * emit the test and skip the call entirely - which is exactly why the ten
 * uncovered opcodes at the bottom of this file are uncovered, and why
 * jump_if_false is NOT among them.
 *
 * Three rows exist for performance rather than for semantics, and deleting them
 * would be correct and slow:
 *
 *   - the site pointer on the property and call helpers, which is where Phase
 *     26's inline caches attach WITHOUT an ABI break. A register machine gives
 *     `get_prop dst, obj, name` a stable identity, and that identity is the
 *     whole reason the bytecode is a register machine rather than a stack one.
 *   - the literal memos on ct_aot_new_string and ct_aot_new_bigint_literal.
 *     These are NOT an optimisation an image may skip: context::allocate counts
 *     TOTAL LIFETIME allocations against a 40,000,000 ceiling and nothing ever
 *     resets it, so an un-memoised literal in a hot loop reaches an UNCATCHABLE
 *     raise on a program the interpreter runs forever.
 *   - ct_aot_failed, which is one load of one bool so that the failure poll can
 *     sit on back edges and before returns rather than after every allocation.
 *     That is sound because allocate() still returns a valid object past the
 *     ceiling: running on is wasteful, never unsafe.
 *
 * And the standing performance HAZARD, which Phase 4 owns: is_safepoint means
 * the caller's live values must be reachable across the call, and the cheapest
 * way to guarantee that - spill everything to a shadow frame at every call
 * site - would give back much of what compiling won. Precise rooting is Phase
 * 29 for that reason. Do not let the conservative version become the ABI.
 *
 * DERIVED, NOT INVENTED. Every row was proposed against the handler in
 * lib/Script/vm/ that owns its semantics today, then independently refuted by a
 * second reader against the same code, then merged. Where a row disagrees with
 * what an opcode's name suggests, its comment says why.
 *
 * AND CITE BY NAME, NOT BY LINE. A line number in a comment is a fact with an
 * expiry date. Phases 3, 4 and 5 moved several hundred lines of run_loop.cpp,
 * call.cpp and vm.hpp, and the DELEGATES TO citations went stale WHOLESALE:
 * six of them ended up pointing past the end of a file that had shrunk, and
 * many more still land inside their file while naming a handler that has since
 * moved elsewhere in it. `run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(cell_get)` cannot drift; the
 * line range that row used to carry did, and pointed past the end of the file.
 * (Written out rather than quoted, because the checker below cannot tell a
 * citation from a mention of one - which is the right way round.)
 *
 * ctcompile_def_citations checks the half a machine can see - a citation past
 * the end of the file it names - and the rows repaired for it were rewritten
 * as names rather than given fresh numbers to rot. The other half is on the
 * reader: IF YOU FOLLOW A LINE CITATION HERE AND FIND SOMETHING ELSE, the row
 * is stale rather than wrong about the semantics; the claims were checked, the
 * addresses were not re-checked afterwards.
 */

/* A consumer defines whichever of the two it wants and ignores the other; both
 * are UNDEFINED at the bottom of this file, so every inclusion starts clean and
 * a translation unit can include it twice - once for the helpers and once for
 * the coverage - which is exactly what ctcompile does. Without that, the
 * fallback below survives the first include and collides with the second
 * definition, which is a -Wmacro-redefined error and not an obvious one. */
#ifndef CT_AOT_HELPER
#define CT_AOT_HELPER(name, ret, params, may_throw, may_reenter, is_safepoint)
#endif
#ifndef CT_AOT_COVERS
#define CT_AOT_COVERS(helper, opcode)
#endif


/* Declared in Phase 2 because every other row's first parameter has no
 * producer without it, and because vm.hpp:954 writes registers_[base+slot]
 * UNCONDITIONALLY for the winning handler - so the register span must exist
 * before the first ct_aot_handler_push, which is an ABI shape fact, not a
 * Phase 4 detail. `storage` is caller-allocated of CT_AOT_FRAME_BYTES so
 * the layout stays Phase 4's and stays changeable. Keeping the real proto
 * (not nullptr) is required or current_stack (vm.hpp:426-449) skips the
 * frame and every AOT frame vanishes from .stack.
 * DELEGATES TO: NEW, Phase 4 owns the body: push a call_frame carrying the
 *   image's real function_proto, reserve reg_count slots in
 *   context::registers_ (GC root 2), bump a new native_depth_ so the 512
 *   guard (call.cpp:77) counts AOT frames, and consume-and-clear
 *   pending_new_target_ exactly as call.cpp:90-91 does.
 * FAILURE: Returns NULL on the 512-depth raise; caller returns
 *   CT_AOT_FAILED without leaving. Never throws catchably.
 *
 * `receiver` WAS ADDED IN PHASE 4, and it is a rooting fix rather than a
 * convenience. The interpreted path stores the receiver in call_frame::receiver
 * and objects.cpp marks it for every live frame; the compiled path stored it
 * nowhere, so a compiled body's `this` was reachable from NO root. For an
 * ordinary call that was survivable by accident - the receiver is usually still
 * in the caller's register - but for `new` it was not: op::construct's fresh
 * instance lives in a C++ local until the body returns, and the callee's own
 * register still holds the callee. A collection at any safepoint inside a
 * compiled constructor freed the object being constructed. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_enter, /* ret */ struct ct_aot_frame *,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_ctx *ctx, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint32_t reg_count, uint64_t receiver, void *storage),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* THE FRAME'S OWN SLOTS, which is how a compiled body keeps a value alive
 * across a safepoint. Phase 4.
 *
 * ct_aot_enter reserves reg_count slots in context::registers_ - GC root 2,
 * traced in full - and until this row there was no way to ADDRESS them. So a
 * body had nowhere to put a live value except a C++ local, and the header of
 * this file is explicit that a value living only in a native frame is
 * reachable from NONE of the collector's roots. Every helper marked
 * is_safepoint was therefore unusable by any body holding anything.
 *
 * THE POINTER IS VALID UNTIL THE NEXT SAFEPOINT AND NOT ONE INSTRUCTION
 * LONGER. registers_ is a std::vector and a nested call resizes it, so the
 * base moves; this is the same rule the interpreter's own reg() follows by
 * recomputing registers_.data() + base on every access, and a backend that
 * hoists this call out of a loop containing a safepoint has miscompiled.
 *
 * It is deliberately NOT a stack-allocated shadow frame with its own chain,
 * which is what a generic version of this phase would build: vm.hpp writes
 * registers_[base + slot] UNCONDITIONALLY for a winning handler, so the
 * landing slot for a catch has to live in the register file. The ct_aot_enter
 * row says so, and two shadow stacks would be two things to keep in step.
 * DELEGATES TO: NEW, Phase 4 owns the body: registers_.data() + the span
 *   ct_aot_enter recorded for this frame.
 * FAILURE: None. The span exists for the life of the frame, and its slots
 *   were initialised to undefined by ct_aot_enter - not left uninitialised,
 *   because a collection before a slot is first assigned would otherwise
 *   trace whatever was in the vector. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_slots, /* ret */ uint64_t *,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* The handler truncation is a RESIZE, not a pop, so ct_aot_handler_pop is
 * not it and must not be used for it - a `return` out of a try otherwise
 * leaves its catch reachable from the caller, silently. Must NOT run on the
 * CT_AOT_UNWOUND path (the unwinder already truncated and destroyed the
 * frame) and is a harmless no-op on CT_AOT_FAILED (the search emptied
 * handlers_).
 * DELEGATES TO: pop the frame, truncate handlers_ to
 *   call_frame::handler_base exactly as op::ret does
 *   (run_loop.cpp:1039-1041), decrement native_depth_, release the register
 *   span.
 * FAILURE: None. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_leave, /* ret */ void, /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* There is a universal DELIVERY point and no universal DETECTION point:
 * nothing sets an 'unwind pending' flag, and the interpreter notices only
 * because VM_NEXT re-derives the frame and re-tests. A native frame has no
 * loop head. Exported rather than inlined because an AOT->AOT direct call
 * bypasses every helper, and only the caller can reclassify a callee's
 * status for ITS frame (a callee's UNWOUND is the caller's CAUGHT when the
 * caller owns the winning handler). One implementation is also the only way
 * the two projects cannot disagree about the precedence order.
 * DELEGATES TO: the run-loop head's own exit test, VM_NEXT
 *   (run_loop.cpp:94-103 `if (frames_.size() <= stop_depth || failed_) goto
 *   vm_done`), plus context::failed() and the state unwind_to_handler
 *   leaves: frames_.size() after the resize (vm.hpp:950) and call_frame::ip
 *   after the assignment (vm.hpp:952).
 * FAILURE: THE CLASSIFIER. Precedence is the run-loop head's, in order: (1)
 *   frames_.size() < fr depth => CT_AOT_UNWOUND (the call_frame is
 *   DESTROYED, so this must be first - every later test dereferences it);
 *   (2) failed_ => CT_AOT_FAILED (before the caught test, because VM_NEXT
 *   tests failed_ in the same disjunction and leaves regardless of a
 *   handler that just fired); (3) CT_AOT_PAD_BIT set in fr->ip =>
 *   CT_AOT_CAUGHT; (4) CT_AOT_OK. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_check, /* ret */ int32_t, /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* MERGED: the objects and frames areas proposed identical rows; the frames
 * spelling wins and both `covers` lists are emptied under the partition
 * rule (new_object/new_array/own_keys/instance_of are owned by their own
 * rows). VERIFIED that allocate() (vm.hpp:223-233) raises past the 40M
 * ceiling and then STILL returns a valid linked object - so a compiled
 * function running past the ceiling is WASTEFUL, NEVER UNSAFE. That is what
 * licenses the poll discipline: back-edges and before returns only, not
 * after every allocation. Return is uint32_t, not int32_t, under the
 * return-type rule - it is data, not a status. Kept separate from
 * ct_aot_check because the back-edge poll wants one load of one bool, not a
 * frame-depth comparison.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::failed() (vm.hpp:453) over the flag context::raise
 *   sets (vm.hpp:932-936).
 * FAILURE: IT IS THE SIGNAL. Nonzero means the run is over: no try/catch
 *   can see it, no landing pad exists, the only correct response is to
 *   return CT_AOT_FAILED up the whole native chain. Sticky until
 *   context::take_error(), which belongs to the turn and never runs inside
 *   compiled code. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_failed, /* ret */ uint32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* `slot` is NOT droppable, and that correction is a memory-safety fix
 * rather than a modelling quibble: vm.hpp:954 is `registers_[target.base +
 * h.slot] = thrown_` and it is UNCONDITIONAL for whatever frame wins,
 * native or interpreted, guarded above only by `registers_.size() <
 * h.reg_top`. With slot defaulting to 0 and an AOT base at
 * registers_.size() that is an out-of-bounds write that would not even fail
 * loudly, because context::call over-allocates every window by 8 slots
 * (call.cpp:73). Once the frame reserves a real delivery slot,
 * unwind_to_handler is correct for a native target UNCHANGED - `pad` rides
 * in handler::address and lands in call_frame::ip. slot must be <= 65535
 * (handler::slot is uint16_t) and base+slot < reg_top. THE HELPER MUST READ
 * registers_.size() ITSELF: this is the only opcode capturing the high-
 * water mark ABSOLUTELY, so it may not be hoisted out of a loop or sunk
 * past anything that pushes registers.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::handlers_.push_back(handler{frames_.size()-1, pad,
 *   registers_.size(), slot}) - run_loop.cpp:1490-1494; the handler struct
 *   is vm.hpp:746-751.
 * FAILURE: None. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_handler_push, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint32_t pad, uint32_t slot),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_handler_push, /* opcode */ push_handler)

/* It pops the GLOBALLY innermost handler without consulting
 * call_frame::handler_base, so a mis-balanced emission silently drops a
 * CALLER's catch and nothing reports it. Balance is a compiler invariant
 * only - compile_break and compile_continue each emit one pop per open try
 * when leaving the block (statements.cpp:282-284, 294-296) - and Phase 10
 * inherits that obligation on every non-local exit path. `fr` is carried
 * purely so a debug build can assert handlers_.back().frame == this frame's
 * depth, turning a silent bug class into a loud one. It is NOT the frame-
 * exit truncation; that is ct_aot_leave.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::handlers_.pop_back() - run_loop.cpp:1499-1501, `if
 *   (!handlers_.empty()) { handlers_.pop_back(); }`.
 * FAILURE: None - a no-op on an empty stack, as the handler is. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_handler_pop, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_handler_pop, /* opcode */ pop_handler)

/* THE ONE DESIGN DECISION THAT DISSOLVES THE PLAN'S BIGGEST BLOCKER.
 * Verified: vm.hpp:955 sets `thrown_ = value::undefined()` BEFORE
 * unwind_to_handler returns true, so any design where a helper reports 'a
 * throw is in flight' and hands back thrown_ delivers undefined for every
 * catch in the program. The fix is to stop fighting the VM: let the AOT
 * frame carry the pad id in handler::address and a real delivery register
 * in handler::slot, so all four of unwind_to_handler's steps remain correct
 * for a native target and this becomes a pure READ of what it deposited.
 * Zero change to unwind_to_handler, zero change to the interpreter, and the
 * thrown value is delivered into registers_ (GC root 2) instead of into a
 * native frame reachable from none of the 17 roots. It is the single
 * delivery point for every area, which keeps a pad out-parameter and a
 * value out-parameter off every throwing helper's signature.
 * WAS UNIMPLEMENTABLE AS WRITTEN, found on 2026-08-22 by trying, and
 * IMPLEMENTED IN PHASE 6. The problem was real: this row says to read back
 * registers_[call_frame::base + handler::slot], and unwind_to_handler POPS
 * the handler before it writes, so by the time a compiled body could ask,
 * which register the thrown value went into was unknowable.
 *
 * The row then wrote down two possible fixes and took neither, because
 * "taking one without a compiled `try` to test it would be inventing on no
 * evidence". There is one now - unittests/unit/aot_throw - and the fix
 * taken is a THIRD: call_frame::landed_slot records the slot at the moment
 * unwind_to_handler writes it. Two bytes on a frame the interpreter never
 * reads, and this row's signature - which two code generators are written
 * against - does not move. The row's own second option, adding the slot to
 * these parameters, would have changed that signature to save the two bytes.
 *
 * ct_aot_check returns CT_AOT_CAUGHT now, on CT_AOT_PAD_BIT in ip, and that
 * bit is defined in aot_entry.h with the measurement aot.hpp was waiting
 * for: `ip` is a size_t index into bytecode, and 2^63 instructions would be
 * 74 exabytes, so the top bit cannot collide with a real one.
 *
 * DELEGATES TO: READS BACK what unwind_to_handler already wrote:
 *   call_frame::ip (assigned from handler::address, vm.hpp:952) and
 *   registers_[call_frame::base + handler::slot] (written vm.hpp:954). Then
 *   clears CT_AOT_PAD_BIT in ip. It does NOT perform those two writes.
 * FAILURE: None. Called exactly once, only after CT_AOT_CAUGHT. Clearing
 *   the pad marker makes a second call a debug assert rather than a second
 *   catch. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_catch_land, /* ret */ uint32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t *out_thrown), /* may_throw */ 0,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* may_reenter=1 is the most surprising column in the table and it is real:
 * describe_thrown calls lookup_property(thrown,"name") and ("message")
 * (call.cpp:372-373), which runs an accessor, and anything not
 * is_kind(object) falls to to_string, reaching to_primitive_string's
 * `call(fn,{},v)` on toString then valueOf (coerce.cpp:172-176) - a full
 * nested run_loop. Page JavaScript runs in the middle of formatting a fatal
 * error. Every live value must be spilled across this call EXCEPT `thrown`
 * itself, because the helper's first action is `thrown_ = thrown` and
 * thrown_ is GC root 13 from that instant. The status is not hand-computed
 * here; the body ends in ct_aot_check so this and every other throwing
 * helper classify identically.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(throw_value) - `thrown_ = reg(in.a); if
 *   (!unwind_to_handler()) raise("uncaught " + describe_thrown(thrown_));`
 *   - vm.hpp:945-959, call.cpp:369-386, vm.hpp:932-936 - then ct_aot_check
 *   for the status.
 * FAILURE: BOTH TIERS, AND IT NEVER RETURNS CT_AOT_OK. CT_AOT_CAUGHT: the
 *   winning handler is this frame's - call ct_aot_catch_land once and
 *   branch to the pad. CT_AOT_UNWOUND: the winner is below, frames_ is
 *   already resized past this frame - return immediately, run no epilogue,
 *   write no result, do not call ct_aot_leave. CT_AOT_FAILED: nothing
 *   caught it, and note unwind_to_handler pops as it SEARCHES
 *   (vm.hpp:947-949), so handlers_ is left COMPLETELY EMPTY and thrown_ is
 *   NOT cleared (vm.hpp:955 is only reached on a hit), which is what keeps
 *   the object alive while its own toString runs. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_throw, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t thrown), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_throw, /* opcode */ throw_value)

/* MERGED AWAY a single ct_aot_frame_read(field) reader: a discriminator
 * puts a RUNTIME switch in an out-of-line C function, so the constant
 * cannot fold across the ABI boundary and every `this` in a Phaser method
 * body pays a branch. Four symbols beat one symbol and an immediate, and
 * their LLVM declarations differ anyway. Routing through effective_this
 * rather than reading call_frame::receiver is non-negotiable under
 * Principle 3 - the arrow rule (`is_arrow ? captured_this : receiver`) is
 * the VM's definition, and knowing is_arrow statically is not permission to
 * restate it. frame_receiver is GC root 6, so no new root.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::effective_this(const call_frame &) -
 *   vm.hpp:897-902, via run_loop.cpp:1059-1060.
 * FAILURE: None. There is NO sloppy-mode globalThis substitution: a frame
 *   entered with no receiver hands back raw undefined (op::call sets
 *   receiver = undefined, run_loop.cpp:937), so `this` at top level or in a
 *   bare call is undefined. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_this, /* ret */ uint64_t, /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_this, /* opcode */ load_this)

/* MERGE: the calls area proposed ct_aot_take_new_target, a PROLOGUE
 * consume-and-clear of pending_new_target_. That is a different operation
 * from this opcode, and it belongs to ct_aot_enter (which now performs it),
 * not to a load site. This spelling wins for the opcode because
 * run_loop.cpp:1078 reads the FRAME FIELD, not the pending flag. It must
 * stay a frame read at every use and must never be cached in an SSA value
 * across anything that can suspend or re-enter: context::resume rebuilds
 * the frame WITHOUT copying new_target (call.cpp:673-692), so new.target
 * reads undefined after an await, and a backend that cached it would be
 * more correct than the interpreter and would diverge from it.
 * frame_new_target is GC root 9.
 * DELEGATES TO: call_frame::new_target (vm.hpp:865) -
 *   run_loop.cpp:1077-1078 `reg(in.a) = vm_frame->new_target`.
 * FAILURE: None - undefined for an ordinary call. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_new_target, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_new_target, /* opcode */ load_new_target)

/* Emitted only for a named function expression's self-binding, and
 * immediately followed by new_cell when the name is captured
 * (statements.cpp:737-738), so its result feeds ct_aot_cell_new on the very
 * next instruction. frame_closure is GC root 5, so this is a read of an
 * existing root rather than a new one.
 * DELEGATES TO: call_frame::closure (vm.hpp:851) - run_loop.cpp:1103-1104,
 *   undefined when closure is null.
 * FAILURE: None - undefined in the top-level script frame, which has no
 *   closure. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_callee, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_callee, /* opcode */ load_callee)

/* MERGED from two spellings: the objects area passed the closure
 * explicitly, arguing the handler's implicit frames_.back() would resolve
 * `super` against a stranger's home object for a native frame. That risk is
 * real but disappears once ct_aot_enter puts the AOT frame IN frames_ with
 * its own closure - and Phase 4 must carry closure anyway for op::closure's
 * program selection and GC root 5, so passing it separately duplicates
 * state and creates two sources of 'my closure' that can drift. This
 * spelling wins; the helper reads fr->closure and debug-asserts it equals
 * frames_.back().closure. Two facts keep it a helper: it is NOT a
 * call_frame field but a property lookup over closure_object::props, whose
 * returned pointer is invalidated by any later set() (so the helper copies
 * the value out and never hands a pointer across the ABI); and __home is an
 * ORDINARY observable property installed by op::set_prop (classes.cpp:136,
 * 203), so the home object must never be baked into the image. All-zero
 * flags verified: find() consults `props` only, never `accessors`, so no
 * getter can run.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp:1402-1411 - `closure_object *running =
 *   frames_.back().closure; if (running) if (value *home =
 *   running->find("__home")) reg(a) = *home;`, closure_object::find being
 *   the linear scan at vm.hpp:117-123.
 * FAILURE: None. Undefined when the frame has no closure and undefined when
 *   the closure has no __home. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_home, /* ret */ uint64_t, /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_home, /* opcode */ load_home)

/* MERGED: the calls area passed the value explicitly, `(ctx, uint64_t
 * new_target)`. This zero-operand spelling wins because run_loop.cpp:1374
 * reads THIS frame's new_target, and an explicit parameter would let a
 * backend hand over a stale or cached value - precisely what
 * ct_aot_new_target's row forbids. It must reproduce the LEAK, not repair
 * it: grep confirms exactly three writers in the tree
 * (run_loop.cpp:1021-1022, call.cpp:90-91, run_loop.cpp:1374), and both
 * clearing sites are JS-closure frame pushes, so a native or generator
 * callee leaves the flag set and the next ordinary call anywhere sees a
 * truthy new.target. The helper does the write and nothing else. Adjacency
 * to the ct_aot_call that follows is a lowering invariant the ABI cannot
 * enforce.
 * DELEGATES TO: a one-line context member: `pending_new_target_ = fr's
 *   new_target`, verbatim run_loop.cpp:1374. pending_new_target_ is private
 *   and is GC root 4, so AOT cannot reach it structurally.
 * FAILURE: None. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_pass_new_target, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_pass_new_target, /* opcode */ pass_new_target)

/* MERGE REFUSED: the calls area fused this with gather_rest into one
 * ct_aot_arg_array. They are not one operation - this one has a SECOND
 * EFFECT, writing call_frame::arguments_object (GC root 7), and gather_rest
 * READS that side-slot on one of its two paths. A fusion either drops the
 * write (breaking gather_rest's first path) or adds it to gather_rest
 * (wrong). But the calls area's parameter correction is adopted and is the
 * important half: the interpreter reads the frame's LIVE slots (reg(i) for
 * i < argc), which are the parameter locals themselves, so the ABI must not
 * be founded on 'the incoming argv is immutable' - that holds today only
 * because both opcodes are emitted in the prologue. Hence `slots`, a frame-
 * local GC-rooted mutable array of size max(param_count, argc) that IS
 * where the parameters live. Sequencing: this runs BEFORE the parameter
 * prologue and claims a register an extra argument may sit in, so Phase 10
 * must not sink it below the prologue.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::make_array (vm.hpp:238) + the loop at
 *   run_loop.cpp:1083-1100, INCLUDING the frame side-slot write
 *   `vm_frame->arguments_object = list`.
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY (the allocation ceiling). Plain value return;
 *   poll ct_aot_failed. No JS exception, no unwind. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_make_arguments, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const uint64_t *slots, uint32_t argc),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_make_arguments, /* opcode */ make_arguments)

/* The consumer of the side-slot ct_aot_make_arguments writes; splitting a
 * producer from its only consumer across two areas is how one of them goes
 * missing, so both rows sit here. Both paths must be reproduced, not
 * merged: the handler picks the arguments_object when the body built one,
 * because building it claimed a register an extra argument may have been
 * in, and the compiler happening to emit a == b is not a general invariant.
 * `from` stays its own parameter and is not fused with argc - it indexes
 * the arguments ARRAY on one path and is a frame-slot offset on the other,
 * so it must not be lowered as a pure literal count.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp:774-798 - make_array, then either
 *   call_frame::arguments_object's items[from..] when it is an array, or
 *   registers_[base+i] for i in [from, argc).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY - the allocation ceiling, never a JS exception,
 *   and it does not unwind. Plain value return; poll ct_aot_failed. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_gather_rest, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const uint64_t *slots, uint32_t argc, uint32_t from),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_gather_rest, /* opcode */ gather_rest)

/* new_cell is the only in-out register operand in the set; modelling it
 * VALUE->VALUE removes the register from the ABI so Phase 4 can change the
 * frame layout without touching this file. `init` must be in a GC-visible
 * slot before the call - allocate() does not collect today
 * (collect_if_due's sole caller is Shell/browser.cpp:307, 'between
 * callbacks'), but the interpreter is safe here only because its argument
 * came out of registers_, and this helper's argument is reachable from none
 * of the 17 roots.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::allocate<cell_object>(value) - vm.hpp:223 via
 *   run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(new_cell); cell_object in vm.hpp.
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY, and non-diverting: allocate() raises past the
 *   ceiling and STILL returns a valid cell, so this returns a value plainly
 *   and never a status. Poll ct_aot_failed at back-edges. No try/catch can
 *   see it and there is no landing pad for it. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_cell_new, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t init), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_cell_new, /* opcode */ new_cell)

/* Constraint 1 makes this a helper and not a lowered load: heap_object has
 * a virtual destructor (value.hpp:167), so the vtable pointer sits at
 * offset 0 and `kind` is not where a structural emitter would put it. No
 * handle at all, which lets the LLVM declaration carry
 * memory(read)+nounwind+willreturn so a repeated read folds within a
 * straight line but never across a call that could run user JS. It must NOT
 * be memory(argmem:read) or readnone - the argument is an i64, not a
 * pointer, so the only honest statement is a whole-memory read. It owns
 * get_upvalue under the partition rule: the fused opcode is 'fetch the
 * cell, then the cell op', and ct_aot_upvalue_cell supplies the fetch.
 * Third producer worth knowing: compile/frames.cpp:141-149 marks every
 * IMPORTED binding boxed and deliberately emits no new_cell, so this is
 * also the read path for every `import`ed name.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(cell_get) - `reg(b).is_kind(heap_kind::cell) ?
 *   static_cast<cell_object*>(reg(b).as_heap())->slot :
 *   value::undefined()`.
 * FAILURE: None, and that is a semantic guarantee: a non-cell argument
 *   yields undefined silently, so a mis-emitted boxing decision produces a
 *   wrong value rather than a trap. NOT a TDZ check - an uninitialised
 *   `let` reads as undefined. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_cell_get, /* ret */ uint64_t, /* params */ (uint64_t cell),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_cell_get, /* opcode */ cell_get)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_cell_get, /* opcode */ get_upvalue)

/* The write half of the same primitive, split from the read for the same
 * vtable reason. No write barrier exists or is needed today: collect()
 * (objects.cpp:434-511) is a full mark from precise roots followed by a
 * sweep of the intrusive list, not incremental - but see the GC note in
 * `notes` for why this row is the one that would grow a frame parameter if
 * that changes. Its declaration must NOT be argmemonly-with-a-known-object:
 * it writes memory an arbitrary closure can observe, so it invalidates any
 * cached ct_aot_cell_get. Because a module export cell is shared, this is
 * also how a compiled module writes a binding an interpreted importer
 * reads.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(cell_set) - `if
 *   (reg(a).is_kind(heap_kind::cell))
 *   static_cast<cell_object*>(reg(a).as_heap())->slot = reg(b)`.
 * FAILURE: None. A non-cell target drops the store silently - an assignment
 *   that does nothing at all - and AOT must reproduce that rather than
 *   diagnose it. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_cell_set, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (uint64_t cell, uint64_t v), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_cell_set, /* opcode */ cell_set)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_cell_set, /* opcode */ set_upvalue)

/* `covers` is EMPTY under the partition rule - get_upvalue and set_upvalue
 * are owned by the cell rows - but the decomposition is verified line by
 * line against both handlers and composes exactly. It costs nothing new
 * because op::closure's builder already needs the same fetch
 * (run_loop.cpp:906-909), and an AOT frame with no register window cannot
 * let a closure helper reach into it. `index` is typed uint32 and
 * explicitly not a register so the register allocator is physically unable
 * to renumber it - set_upvalue's `a` operand is an UPVALUE INDEX, the one
 * opcode in the set whose first operand is not a frame slot. Note the
 * asymmetry the helper erases: get_upvalue's index is in `b`, set_upvalue's
 * is in `a`.
 * DELEGATES TO: call_frame::closure (vm.hpp:851) + closure_object::upvalues
 *   (vm.hpp:160) - the guarded fetch in run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(get_upvalue) and
 *   1551-1561.
 * FAILURE: None, by returning undefined for all three miss cases the
 *   handlers already have: no closure (a top-level script frame), index >=
 *   upvalues.size(), or an entry that is not a cell. Feeding that undefined
 *   to ct_aot_cell_get/ct_aot_cell_set reproduces the fused opcodes
 *   EXACTLY, because both also no-op on a non-cell. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_upvalue_cell, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint32_t index), /* may_throw */ 0,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* Verified: string_hash and string_equal both declare is_transparent
 * (containers.hpp:64,87), so find(std::string_view) hits the heterogeneous
 * path and allocates nothing - the image can point straight at its own
 * static string data with no runtime name table. The operand is an sidx
 * into proto->names, NOT constants (bytecode.hpp:52 gets this wrong);
 * passing text sidesteps the disagreement. THE ABI MUST FORBID NOW: no
 * `uint64_t hash` parameter so ctcompile can bake the hash in. string_hash
 * is boost::hash over a string_view and containers.hpp:130-133 says of
 * exactly this, 'Using anything else here is a lookup that silently never
 * matches' - a hash baked by a different Boost version is not a wrong
 * answer, it is a global that is permanently undefined.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::globals_ (string_flat_map<value>, vm.hpp:1043;
 *   containers.hpp:126) - run_loop.cpp:225-231.
 * FAILURE: None, and the absence is load-bearing: an undeclared global does
 *   NOT throw ReferenceError, it silently yields undefined. `foo` and
 *   `typeof foo` are the same instruction, and feature detection written
 *   against a ReferenceError gets undefined instead. A spec-conformant
 *   backend that emitted a throw would break pages the interpreter runs. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_global_get, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const char *name, uint32_t name_len),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_global_get, /* opcode */ get_global)

/* Cannot share with the read: different direction, different arity. Two
 * things it must NOT become. (1) It bypasses accessors - if a binding layer
 * mirrors globals through a window object with setters, this does not fire
 * them - so it stays a raw map store and never grows into store_property.
 * (2) `a` is the SOURCE operand on an opcode whose name says destination
 * (writes_a is false), so the value comes last and there is no result. The
 * DEFINE body must be find-then-assign, constructing a std::string only on
 * insertion, keeping the update path allocation-free like the
 * interpreter's; boost::unordered_flat_map's operator[] is not guaranteed
 * heterogeneous. That insertion is a C++ allocation, not a GC one, so
 * allocates and is_safepoint stay false - but a std::bad_alloc must
 * terminate at this boundary, not unwind through a C-ABI frame.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::globals_ - run_loop.cpp:234-236
 *   `globals_[vm_proto->names[in.bx()]] = reg(in.a)`.
 * FAILURE: None. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_global_set, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const char *name, uint32_t name_len, uint64_t v),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_global_set, /* opcode */ set_global)

/* VERIFIED, AND IT IS THE ONE ROW WHERE 'the memo is just an optimisation'
 * would be a semantic bug. I grepped `allocations_` across lib/Script: it
 * is incremented in allocate() (vm.hpp:224) and appears NOWHERE else -
 * never reset by collect(), take_error() or execute(). The 40,000,000
 * ceiling therefore counts TOTAL allocations for the process lifetime, not
 * live objects. Interpreted, a string literal in a per-pixel loop allocates
 * ONE object for the whole run because run_loop.cpp:180-189 memoises it;
 * the same loop AOT-compiled without the memo allocates one per iteration
 * and at 480k pixels a frame reaches the ceiling in about a second, raising
 * an UNCATCHABLE failure on a program the interpreter runs forever. That is
 * a divergence in the raise tier introduced by an optimisation, so the memo
 * is part of the ABI. SITE == NULL MEANS DO NOT MEMOISE, which is what lets
 * ct_aot_type_of_name's companion allocation stay at parity
 * (VM_CASE(type_of) has no cache at all). Pointer+length rather than a C
 * string because function_proto::strings is std::vector<std::string> and a
 * JS string may contain an embedded NUL, so `a\0b` would silently truncate.
 * String identity is unobservable (strict_equals compares text), so the
 * memo is safe; it is the ceiling, not identity, that makes it mandatory.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::string (vm.hpp:234) ->
 *   context::allocate<string_object> (vm.hpp:223), THROUGH string_cache_
 *   (vm.hpp:984, GC root 15, marked objects.cpp:481-484, cleared by
 *   context::execute at call.cpp:230). MUST BE EXTRACTED: the memo lives
 *   inline in VM_CASE(load_string) at run_loop.cpp:178-192 and its key must
 *   widen from `const function_proto *` to `const void *`. Proposed lift:
 *   context::interned_string(const void *site, uint32_t slot,
 *   std::string_view text).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY - never a catchable throw. The sole failure is
 *   allocate()'s ceiling raise, which sets failed_ without entering
 *   unwind_to_handler and is invisible to a JS try. The returned value is a
 *   well-formed string even after the raise, because allocate() still
 *   constructs and returns the object. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_new_string, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint32_t slot, const char *utf8, uint32_t len),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_new_string, /* opcode */ load_string)

/* Same verified ceiling argument as ct_aot_new_string: bigint_cache_ is
 * what keeps a BigInt literal in a loop from charging the monotonic counter
 * once per iteration. Unlike ct_aot_new_string there is no null-site arm,
 * because every load_bigint site is memoised in the interpreter and nothing
 * else materialises a bigint literal. Takes the SOURCE TEXT, not digits:
 * the parse is bigint_from_literal's and must not be duplicated in
 * ctcompile or `0x1fn`, `0b..n` and the 1.5n->0n substitution drift.
 * Identity is unobservable (strict_equals compares digits), so only the
 * ceiling makes the memo required.
 * DELEGATES TO: bigint_from_literal (bigint.hpp:43) +
 *   allocate<bigint_object> (vm.hpp:223), THROUGH bigint_cache_
 *   (vm.hpp:987, GC root 16, marked objects.cpp:487-490, cleared
 *   call.cpp:231). MUST BE EXTRACTED from VM_CASE(load_bigint) at
 *   run_loop.cpp:154-177 into context::interned_bigint_literal(const void
 *   *site, uint32_t slot, std::string_view text).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY. A literal the lexer accepted but that is not an
 *   integer (1.5n) does NOT throw - `parsed.value_or(bigint{0})` at
 *   run_loop.cpp:169 substitutes 0n. The only failure is the allocation
 *   ceiling; a well-formed value is returned on every path. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_new_bigint_literal, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint32_t slot, const char *literal, uint32_t len),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_new_bigint_literal, /* opcode */ load_bigint)

/* Returns an OPAQUE POINTER rather than a struct by value, because
 * prehashed_name is three words and constraint 5 admits only scalars and
 * pointers; ctbrowser's DEFINE side reconstructs the prehashed_name behind
 * the pointer. `covers` is EMPTY: in the original it claimed five opcodes
 * that their own rows also claimed, and duplicate claims plus one silent
 * omission is exactly the state that lets a coverage checker pass while a
 * hole exists. The hash MUST come from hash_name and never from anything
 * ctcompile computes - containers.hpp says outright 'Using anything else
 * here is a lookup that silently never matches' - so the handle is produced
 * at image init, never baked. The payoff is already built:
 * object_object::find(prehashed_name) exists and lookup_property already
 * constructs the key once to reuse across the chain, with the comment
 * beside it measuring '2.25 hashes per property access on a Phaser frame'.
 * GLOBAL AND IMMORTAL is safe here and the reason does not transfer to
 * ct_aot_ic: a name record contains no heap pointer and is never GC-traced,
 * so it is context-independent by construction, whereas an IC caches
 * receivers.
 * DELEGATES TO: NEW runtime-side immortal pool. The record OWNS its text:
 *   {std::string text; std::size_t hash;} with hash == core/containers.hpp
 *   hash_name(text). It is NOT prehashed_name itself - that is
 *   {std::string_view, std::size_t}, a NON-OWNING view, and a pool built
 *   out of it dangles. The pool hands out a prehashed_name constructed over
 *   the record it owns.
 * FAILURE: None - it cannot fail, is not a GC value, and interning is
 *   idempotent. It may malloc; a C++ bad_alloc escaping a C-ABI helper must
 *   terminate at this boundary. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_intern_name, /* ret */ const struct ct_aot_name *,
              /* params */ (const char *utf8, uint32_t len), /* may_throw */ 0,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* COVERAGE FIX, and it is the one genuine GAP the five area proposals left.
 * jump_if_false and jump_if_true were claimed by nobody: the values area
 * covered only logical_not and listed the two jumps as 'shared with control
 * flow', while the control-flow rows never appeared. Reading
 * run_loop.cpp:692 and 701 confirms both call truthy(reg(in.a)), so they
 * are not no-helper opcodes and rule (a) would have failed. All three now
 * sit on one row. It must remain a call rather than a tag test because
 * coerce.cpp:34-47 reads string_object::text and bigint_object::digits, and
 * AOT must never inspect a heap_object structurally (constraint 1).
 * ToBoolean never invokes valueOf, so every risk column is false and it may
 * carry readonly/nounwind/willreturn. logical_not is then
 * value::boolean(!r), a select over tag_true/tag_false needing no helper;
 * the two jumps are a branch on the result.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::truthy (static, declared vm.hpp:486, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:34). Already a function; no extraction needed.
 * FAILURE: Infallible - no status, no handle, no out-pointer. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_truthy, /* ret */ uint32_t, /* params */ (uint64_t v),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_truthy, /* opcode */ logical_not)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_truthy, /* opcode */ jump_if_false)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_truthy, /* opcode */ jump_if_true)

/* The original signature overloaded the status register - its own stated
 * rule was 'the return value is ALWAYS the status' and it then returned a
 * length in the same int32 slot, colliding only by the coincidence that the
 * shortest answer is six bytes and the tiers are 0/1/2. The return-type
 * rule in `notes` generalises the fix: signed int32 return means status,
 * unsigned means data. The split itself is the win the inventory asks for -
 * classification (pure) apart from allocation (ct_aot_new_string) - so
 * `typeof x === 'function'` becomes a length compare plus memcmp with ZERO
 * allocation where run_loop.cpp:1053 allocates a heap string per execution.
 * Safe forever because coerce.cpp:183-191 returns string_view over string
 * literals, so no interning table and no new GC root. This is the row that
 * forces UNION rather than per-helper checking of the flags rule: (0,0,0,0)
 * here OR ct_aot_new_string's (1,1,0,1) is exactly type_of's inventory row.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::type_of (static, declared vm.hpp:511, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:182-192). Already a function; no extraction needed.
 * FAILURE: Infallible. The return slot carries a LENGTH, and under the
 *   return-type rule an unsigned return is data, never a status. *out_text
 *   points to static storage with unbounded lifetime. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_type_of_name, /* ret */ uint32_t,
              /* params */ (uint64_t v, const char **out_text), /* may_throw */ 0,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_type_of_name, /* opcode */ type_of)
/* AND ct_aot_new_string, because `typeof` produces a VALUE and this helper
 * produces a `const char *`. Two helpers for one opcode on purpose: the common
 * shape `typeof x === "number"` never materialises a string at all - the
 * backend compares the C string and skips the allocation entirely - and only a
 * `typeof` whose result escapes into a variable pays for one. A single helper
 * returning a value would have made the allocation unconditional. */
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_new_string, /* opcode */ type_of)

/* Returns a DOUBLE, not a value: run_loop.cpp:381 is
 * value::number(to_number_value(...)), a bit_cast needing no runtime call,
 * and nan("") is 0x7FF8... which cannot match qnan_mask 0x7FFC...
 * (value.hpp:46,101), so the NaN result can never alias a tagged value.
 * This is unary `+` only (expressions.cpp:340) and is a genuine conversion,
 * not the move it used to be. Deliberately distinct from ct_aot_negate even
 * though both go through to_number_value, because this one's RESULT is
 * provably never heap so the backend can skip the shadow-frame store for
 * it. It cannot skip it for the ARGUMENT.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::to_number_value (declared vm.hpp:518, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:151-169). Already a function; no extraction needed.
 * FAILURE: Full status. CT_AOT_CAUGHT/CT_AOT_UNWOUND on the BigInt
 *   TypeError (coerce.cpp:156-158, reachable here - `+1n` throws where `1n
 *   + 1n` does not) and on a user valueOf/toString throwing. CT_AOT_FAILED
 *   on the 512-frame `call stack exhausted` (call.cpp:77-80) and the
 *   allocation ceiling. *out is NaN on both failure paths: coerce.cpp:158
 *   returns nan("") after the throw, and the exhausted-stack path returns
 *   undefined, which to_number maps to nan. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_to_number, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t v, double *out), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_to_number, /* opcode */ to_number)

/* Shipped for a Principle 3 reason rather than a performance one. This is
 * the NON-re-entering ToNumber that op::add and the six bitwise opcodes
 * actually use, and it is not std::stod: coerce.cpp:54-59 routes a string
 * through string_to_number precisely because stod reads LC_NUMERIC for the
 * decimal separator, throws to report failure, and accepts neither '+5' nor
 * the radix prefixes - I read the comment in place. Without this row, a
 * backend wanting `x + 1` without a call must re-derive string-to-number in
 * ctcompile, which is the second JavaScript engine Principle 3 forbids.
 * Covers no opcode and is inert in both drift checks.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::to_number (static, declared vm.hpp:504, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:49-61).
 * FAILURE: Infallible - no status, no handle. NaN is the failure answer and
 *   is a legitimate result, never a sentinel. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_to_number_primitive, /* ret */ double, /* params */ (uint64_t v),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* Shipped with ct_aot_to_uint32 as the pair that lets a backend inline the
 * six bitwise opcodes with no call, no exception edge and no safepoint once
 * both operands are known Numbers - the elision ct_aot_binary_op_static's
 * may_reenter=0 exists to authorise, and unreachable without these rows.
 * ECMA-262 ToInt32 is NOT a C cast: vm.hpp:497-503 maps every non-finite to
 * 0, truncates toward zero and wraps with std::fmod against 4294967296.0,
 * and the inventory records the two results a naive lowering gets wrong -
 * `-1 >>> 0` is 4294967295, not -1, and `2.7 | 0` is 2.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::to_int32 (static, vm.hpp:494-496, over to_uint32).
 * FAILURE: Infallible. THE ONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION to the return-type
 *   rule, called out here and in the .def header: a signed int32 return
 *   that is DATA, not a status, because ToInt32's result is signed by
 *   definition. It takes no frame handle, which is the mechanical tell - no
 *   status-returning helper in the table lacks one. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_to_int32, /* ret */ int32_t, /* params */ (uint64_t v),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* The other half of the pair, and the one the shift opcodes need on their
 * own: run_loop.cpp:565, 578 and 597 all mask the shift count with
 * `to_uint32(...) & 31U`, and ushr's result is
 * static_cast<double>(to_uint32(b) >> ...) - unsigned - where shr at
 * run_loop.cpp:578 is an arithmetic shift on int32. A backend inlining the
 * two identically produces the wrong answer for negative left operands.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::to_uint32 (static, vm.hpp:497-503).
 * FAILURE: Infallible - no status, no handle. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_to_uint32, /* ret */ uint32_t, /* params */ (uint64_t v),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* Shipped in the first cut rather than deferred, because the alternative is
 * worse than a missing optimisation: an ABSENT helper is an invitation to
 * emit llvm.pow, and C99 F.10.4.4 defines pow(+-1, y) as exactly 1 for
 * EVERY y while coerce.cpp:71-74 returns NaN for `1 ** NaN`, `(-1) **
 * Infinity` and `(-1) ** -Infinity`. The resulting number is one a page
 * keeps computing with where NaN is one isNaN catches. Covers no opcode, so
 * it is invisible to rule (a) and contributes to no union under rule (b) -
 * an empty covers list is inert in both checks.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::exponentiate (static, declared vm.hpp:509, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:70-76).
 * FAILURE: Infallible - no status, no handle. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_exponentiate, /* ret */ double,
              /* params */ (double base, double exponent), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)

/* Cannot be lowered as ct_aot_to_number plus an fneg: run_loop.cpp:366-368
 * returns a freshly ALLOCATED bigint_object on the bigint arm (-0n is 0n; a
 * BigInt has one zero), so the result may be heap and the caller must treat
 * it as an unrooted heap value. That is precisely why this is a value-
 * returning helper and ct_aot_to_number is a double-returning one.
 * DELEGATES TO: to_number_value (vm/coerce.cpp:151) +
 *   allocate<bigint_object> (vm.hpp:223). MUST BE EXTRACTED from
 *   VM_CASE(negate) at run_loop.cpp:363-379 into
 *   context::negate_value(value).
 * FAILURE: Full status. The reachable catchable path is a user
 *   valueOf/toString throwing; to_number_value's own BigInt TypeError is
 *   UNREACHABLE from here because run_loop.cpp:365 tests
 *   is_kind(heap_kind::bigint) first, and an object whose valueOf RETURNS a
 *   bigint is skipped by the `produced.is_heap() && !produced.is_string()`
 *   continue at coerce.cpp:165. CT_AOT_FAILED on stack exhaustion and the
 *   ceiling. *out is written on every path. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_negate, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t v, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_negate, /* opcode */ negate)

/* Kept out of the binary family because it is unary and because its failure
 * profile is strictly weaker than the binary bitwise ops', which DO throw
 * catchably (bigint_binary's mixed-type TypeError at coerce.cpp:283,
 * shl/shr RangeErrors at coerce.cpp:309-310, ushr's unconditional TypeError
 * at coerce.cpp:315). Merging would give bit_not a spurious exception edge
 * and force a landing pad at every `~x`. No ToInt32 step on the BigInt arm
 * - a BigInt has no width to truncate to - so the two arms genuinely
 * differ. It keeps a status return despite being raise-tier-only, because
 * the row is a safepoint whose result may be an unrooted heap bigint; see
 * the status rule in `notes`.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::to_int32 (vm.hpp:494, over to_uint32 at
 *   vm.hpp:497) + bigint operator~ + allocate<bigint_object>. MUST BE
 *   EXTRACTED from VM_CASE(bit_not) at run_loop.cpp:601-614 into
 *   context::bit_not_value(value).
 * FAILURE: CT_AOT_OK or CT_AOT_FAILED only - NEVER a catchable throw. `~1n`
 *   computes (run_loop.cpp:604-606) and `~obj` is -1 (to_int32 -> to_uint32
 *   -> STATIC to_number gives NaN, which to_uint32 maps to 0 at
 *   vm.hpp:499), so neither arm has a JS-catchable throw; may_throw is
 *   purely the ceiling on the BigInt arm. An AOT backend needs a FAULT edge
 *   here, not an exception edge, and only on the BigInt arm. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_bit_not, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t v, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_bit_not, /* opcode */ bit_not)

/* THE NON-RE-ENTERING FAMILY, honest because all seven inventory rows are
 * byte-identical (1,1,1,0,1). These seven use only the STATIC conversions,
 * which skip the valueOf/toString walk, so may_reenter is 0 and a backend
 * that has proven both operands are Numbers may drop the call, the
 * exception edge AND the safepoint. PARAMETER NAMED op_kind, NOT op, and
 * that is not cosmetic: the .def is expanded on ctbrowser's DEFINE side
 * inside namespace ctbrowser::script where `op` is the enum type, so a
 * parameter of that name shadows it and the body cannot spell op::add -
 * context::bigint_binary already dodges this by naming its parameter
 * `kind`. `add` belongs HERE, not with add_generic: compile_binary maps
 * source `+` to add_generic and op::add comes only from `++` and three
 * internal counters, so `x++` on {valueOf:()=>3} is NaN and never runs user
 * code. That is a deviation from the specification, not an optimisation,
 * and if ctbrowser ever fixes it these seven move to ct_aot_binary_op -
 * which rule (b) turns into a build failure in both projects rather than a
 * miscompile.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::bigint_binary (declared vm.hpp:543, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:276, already op-dispatched) + context::to_number (static,
 *   vm.hpp:504/coerce.cpp:49) for add + to_int32/to_uint32 (vm.hpp:494/497)
 *   for the six bitwise. EXTRACTED IN PHASE 5 into
 *   context::binary_op_static(op, value, value) (vm/coerce.cpp), out of the
 *   seven handlers in run_loop.cpp that now call it. DONE.
 * FAILURE: Full status, but the catchable paths reachable from THESE SEVEN
 *   are exactly: bigint_binary's mixed-type TypeError (coerce.cpp:280-286),
 *   the shl and shr 'BigInt shift is too large' RangeErrors
 *   (coerce.cpp:309-310), and ushr's unconditional 'BigInts have no
 *   unsigned right shift' default arm (coerce.cpp:311-317). div/mod/pow
 *   RangeErrors are NOT here - none of those opcodes is in this helper.
 *   CT_AOT_FAILED is the ceiling inside give() (coerce.cpp:289). On a throw
 *   bigint_binary sets *out = undefined; on the pure-Number path there is
 *   no failure at all. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint32_t op_kind, uint64_t lhs, uint64_t rhs, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ add)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ bit_and)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ bit_or)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ bit_xor)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ shl)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ shr)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op_static, /* opcode */ ushr)

/* THE RE-ENTERING FAMILY: all seven rows are byte-identical (1,1,1,1,1) and
 * all seven run page JavaScript through ToPrimitive/ToNumber/ToString, so
 * the caller must have every live value AND both operands reachable from
 * the shadow frame FOR THE DURATION. THE LIFT MUST NOT ROUTE concat THROUGH
 * bigint_binary: coerce.cpp:298-317's switch has no case op::concat, so a
 * lifted binary_op that dispatched everything through bigint_binary first
 * would send `${1n}` to the default arm and throw 'BigInts have no unsigned
 * right shift'. VM_CASE(concat) is unconditionally
 * string(to_string(b)+to_string(c)). The three `+` opcodes STAY THREE: add
 * is a static-family immediate; add_generic does ToPrimitive on both sides
 * then decides, with the string test taking first refusal so `1n + "a"`
 * concatenates while `1n + 1` is a TypeError; concat unconditionally
 * ToStrings both and is emitted only by template literals. Lowering facts
 * the .def must carry: `**` is context::exponentiate, NOT libm; `%` is
 * std::fmod, not an integer srem; `1/0` is Infinity on the Number path but
 * `1n/0n` is a catchable RangeError, so the exception edge cannot be
 * dropped by proving the operands finite.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::to_primitive (vm.hpp:924 PRIVATE, defined
 *   coerce.cpp:125), to_number_value (coerce.cpp:151), to_string
 *   (vm.hpp:510, coerce.cpp:78), exponentiate (vm.hpp:509, coerce.cpp:70),
 *   bigint_binary (coerce.cpp:276), context::string (vm.hpp:234). EXTRACTED IN PHASE 5 into
 *   context::binary_op(op, value, value) (vm/coerce.cpp), out of the seven
 *   handlers in run_loop.cpp that now call it. DONE.
 * FAILURE: Full status. Catchable from any user valueOf/toString, from
 *   to_number_value's BigInt TypeError (coerce.cpp:157), and from
 *   bigint_binary's TypeError/RangeErrors including div's and mod's
 *   'Division by zero' and pow's 'Exponent must be non-negative'
 *   (coerce.cpp:303-305). CT_AOT_FAILED from stack exhaustion
 *   (call.cpp:77-80) and the ceiling. *out is written on every path
 *   (undefined on the bigint throw arms). */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint32_t op_kind, uint64_t lhs, uint64_t rhs, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ sub)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ mul)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ div)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ mod)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ pow)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ add_generic)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_binary_op, /* opcode */ concat)

/* A value member declared noexcept, so no handle is threaded at all. Both
 * opcodes share it and the negation is one xor in the caller. Keeping it
 * OFF ct_aot_loose_equals preserves the split the inventory calls out:
 * compile_binary sends `==`/`!=` to loose_equal/loose_not_equal
 * (expressions.cpp:265-268) and only `===`/`!==` here
 * (expressions.cpp:257-260), so one family helper for all four would attach
 * a throw edge and a safepoint to `switch`, which statements.cpp:523 lowers
 * to a chain of op::equal.
 * DELEGATES TO: value::strict_equals (declared value.hpp:146, defined
 *   inline at value.hpp:271-287; the ABI shim gives it one out-of-line
 *   body).
 * FAILURE: Infallible - no status, no handle. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_strict_equals, /* ret */ uint32_t,
              /* params */ (uint64_t a, uint64_t b), /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_strict_equals, /* opcode */ equal)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_strict_equals, /* opcode */ not_equal)

/* One helper for both opcodes; the negation is one xor in the caller. `out`
 * is a separate uint32 rather than a value because run_loop.cpp:406 wraps
 * it in value::boolean, a select over two constants - and because an int-
 * typed out slot is not a GC-relevant location the caller has to root,
 * which a ct_value out-param would be. AOT SPECIALISATION THAT MATTERS:
 * loose_equal's two hottest emission sites are not `==` at all -
 * expressions.cpp:720 and 848 emit `loose_equal test, base, nullish` for
 * optional chaining, and loose_equals returns at coerce.cpp:195-196 before
 * any to_primitive - so a null/undefined-literal operand makes the site
 * provably non-re-entering and the backend should emit an is_nullish tag
 * test instead of this call. loose_not_equal has no such benign form.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::loose_equals (declared vm.hpp:521, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp:194-258). Already a function; no extraction needed.
 * FAILURE: Full status - catchable from a user valueOf/toString reached
 *   through to_primitive (coerce.cpp:251), CT_AOT_FAILED from stack
 *   exhaustion. *out is 0 or 1 and is written on every path. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_loose_equals, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t a, uint64_t b, uint32_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_loose_equals, /* opcode */ loose_equal)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_loose_equals, /* opcode */ loose_not_equal)

/* FOUR OPCODES, ONE CALL, and the three-way result survives the C ABI
 * because std::partial_ordering is not expressible in it: *out_ordering is
 * CT_AOT_ORD_LESS(-1)/EQUIVALENT(0)/GREATER(1)/UNORDERED(2) and each opcode
 * is a constant compare - is_lteq is (ord==-1||ord==0), false for
 * UNORDERED, which is what makes `NaN <= NaN` false. OPERAND ORDER RE-
 * DERIVED, NOT TAKEN: compile_binary compiles n.a into lhs and n.b into rhs
 * (expressions.cpp:239-296) and run_loop.cpp:672 calls
 * compare_relational(reg(in.b), reg(in.c)) then asks std::is_gt - so `a >
 * b` IS ct_aot_compare(a,b) with ord==GREATER, and lowering `>` as
 * compare(b,a) with ord==LESS runs the two valueOfs in the wrong order
 * (coerce.cpp:327-328) and is observably wrong. Two lowering traps: two
 * strings compare with std::string::compare (coerce.cpp:363), i.e. UTF-8
 * byte order, so a numeric-only fast path silently reintroduces the all-
 * false string comparison; and a BigInt compares EXACTLY against a Number
 * via bigint_compare_double (coerce.cpp:343-348), so this must not fold to
 * a double compare when either side may be a bigint.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::compare_relational (declared vm.hpp:530, defined
 *   vm/coerce.cpp - the row cited 322-374, which is now the middle of
 *   context::binary_op). Already a function; no extraction needed.
 * FAILURE: Full status - catchable from a user valueOf/toString inside
 *   to_primitive, CT_AOT_FAILED from stack exhaustion. *out_ordering is
 *   written on every path (CT_AOT_ORD_UNORDERED on failure). */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_compare, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t lhs, uint64_t rhs, int32_t *out_ordering),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_compare, /* opcode */ less)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_compare, /* opcode */ less_equal)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_compare, /* opcode */ greater)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_compare, /* opcode */ greater_equal)

/* MERGED from two spellings: the values area gave it a status and an int32
 * out-param, the objects area a direct value return. The objects DISCIPLINE
 * wins (raise-tier-only helpers return their result and the caller polls)
 * and the values area's boolean shape wins over a ct_value, because the
 * interpreter's result is value::boolean of a bool and an int out-slot is
 * not a GC location. Under the return-type rule that makes it uint32_t. It
 * was covered by NOTHING in one area's own accounting and its inventory row
 * sits at index 34 inside a contiguous block that area claimed on both
 * sides - the kind of hole rule (a) exists to catch. IT IS THE ONE HELPER
 * THAT ALLOCATES WITHOUT RETURNING AN UNROOTED VALUE: ensure_prototype's
 * fresh object is installed on the ctor closure before it returns
 * (call.cpp:310), so the caller has nothing new to root afterwards. IT
 * MUTATES ITS RIGHT OPERAND - the first `x instanceof F` creates and
 * installs F.prototype - so this is NOT a readonly call and must not be
 * CSE'd or sunk past a write to F.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::ensure_prototype (vm.hpp:926, defined
 *   vm/call.cpp:301-312) + context::implicit_prototypes (vm.hpp:599, const,
 *   non-allocating) + object_object::find. MUST BE EXTRACTED from
 *   VM_CASE(instance_of) at run_loop.cpp:420-477 into
 *   context::instance_of_value(value, value).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY - never a catchable throw, so under the status
 *   rule it returns its boolean plainly and the caller polls ct_aot_failed.
 *   There is no throw_error anywhere in run_loop.cpp:420-477 or in
 *   ensure_prototype: `x instanceof 5`, a non-callable RHS and `x
 *   instanceof someProxy` are all silently FALSE where the specification
 *   demands a TypeError, there is no Symbol.hasInstance, and the chain walk
 *   stops at 64 links. may_throw is purely the ceiling reached through
 *   ensure_prototype -> make_object (call.cpp:308). The result is 0 or 1 on
 *   every path - run_loop.cpp:422 seeds it false first. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_instance_of, /* ret */ uint32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, uint64_t ctor),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_instance_of, /* opcode */ instance_of)

/* MERGED, AND THE PARAMETER ORDER IS THE MERGE. I read the handler: b is
 * the KEY and c is the TARGET (run_loop.cpp:482 tests reg(in.c) for proxy-
 * ness, :491 does to_string(reg(in.b))), so one area proposed (key, target)
 * to mirror the opcode. That loses. There is no uniform operand-to-argument
 * rule to preserve - get_prop is a=dst/b=obj/c=name, set_prop is
 * a=target/b=name/c=value, delete_index is a=target/b=key - so every row
 * needs its own mapping anyway, which the .def's operand columns supply;
 * and in a C ABI where both parameters are uint64_t no type can ever catch
 * a swap. Uniformity across the table is the only defence available, so
 * (target, key) it is, with the operand mapping recorded explicitly in the
 * row. Deliberately NOT merged into any property-read helper: `in` is own-
 * data-only and answers false where a real lookup answers true - a NATIVE
 * right-hand side answers false for everything so `'PI' in Math` is false,
 * an array answers only about integer indices parsed with from_chars
 * requiring the WHOLE key so `'length' in []` and `'1x' in a` are false,
 * and a non-object RHS is false rather than a TypeError. Routing it through
 * lookup_property would silently fix all three and change what pages see.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp:479-515 inline + context::proxy_trap
 *   (vm.hpp:492, call.cpp:351) + context::call (vm.hpp:377) +
 *   context::lookup_property (vm/objects.cpp:218) + context::to_string
 *   (coerce.cpp:78) + std::from_chars for the array-index arm. MUST BE
 *   EXTRACTED into context::has_property_value(value target, value key).
 * FAILURE: Full status. Catchable from a proxy `has` trap running user code
 *   (run_loop.cpp:486), from an accessor or proxy `get` trap through
 *   lookup_property, and from to_string on an object key via
 *   to_primitive_string. CT_AOT_FAILED from stack exhaustion and the
 *   ceiling. *out is 0 or 1 and is written on every path. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_has_property, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, uint64_t key, uint32_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_has_property, /* opcode */ has_property)

/* Handler is `reg(in.a) = make_object();`, row 1,1,1,0,1,0,0. The argument
 * against fusing with new_array into ct_aot_alloc(kind) stands - the
 * discriminator is a compile-time constant at every emission site, so the
 * fusion buys nothing and costs a runtime branch across an ABI boundary.
 * This is the FIRST helper whose result must be published to the shadow
 * frame immediately, because an object literal is new_object followed by a
 * run of set_prop calls that are themselves safepoints.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::make_object (vm.hpp:238, one line over
 *   allocate<object_object>).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY. allocate() (vm.hpp:223-233) raises past the
 *   ceiling and then STILL returns a real object, so the return value is
 *   always well-formed; the caller polls ct_aot_failed at back-edges,
 *   exactly as run_loop's `while (frames_.size() > stop_depth && !failed_)`
 *   tests it once per dispatch rather than once per operation. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_new_object, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr), /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0,
              /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_new_object, /* opcode */ new_object)

/* The element count is NOT statically known at every literal site, and an
 * overstated justification here would invite Phase 26 to trust the hint:
 * expressions.cpp emits new_array at :610 and :909 and appends at
 * :619/:918/:929, but :940 emits op::iterable for a spread element, so `[a,
 * ...b, c]` has no static count, and destructuring.cpp:200's rest pattern
 * has none either. So reserve_hint is a HINT with a defined zero - 0 means
 * 'unknown, reserve nothing' and a non-zero value is a lower bound, never a
 * size. make_array() takes no arguments today so Phase 2's wrapper ignores
 * it entirely, which is still right: adding the parameter later is an ABI
 * break and carrying it costs one register.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::make_array (vm.hpp:238).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY - same as ct_aot_new_object. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_new_array, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint32_t reserve_hint), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_new_array, /* opcode */ new_array)

/* THE HELPER OF THE PROPERTY AREA, and the row where the plan's original
 * status contract died. It claimed a status meaning 'a catchable JS
 * exception is in flight'; that state does not exist. throw_error
 * (vm.hpp:367-370) calls unwind_to_handler INLINE, which resizes frames_,
 * repoints an ip, writes thrown_ into a catch register and CLEARS it
 * (vm.hpp:945-959) - then returns normally. The nested run_loop notices
 * only because its while condition fails and returns value::undefined()
 * at run_loop.cpp's `vm_done:` label, INDISTINGUISHABLE from a getter that returned
 * undefined. What is observable is the frame-count watermark, which is why
 * ct_aot_check exists and why ct_aot_enter must record a depth. THE ct_ic
 * SLOT is kept: object_object is props+index+prototype+accessors with NO
 * shape word (value.hpp:535-540), so a monomorphic IC needs a runtime-side
 * shape word living entirely behind this pointer, and nullability is what
 * makes Phase 26 additive. Its cookie must carry CONTEXT IDENTITY as well
 * as layout version - a per-site mutable static is process-wide,
 * bytecode.hpp:327 anticipates 'an agent per thread', and an IC caching a
 * receiver caches a heap pointer into ONE context's heap. Second-order:
 * object_object::erase REBUILDS `index` (value.hpp:612), so any slot-index
 * cache is invalidated by any delete on the receiver's chain. `out` must
 * point at storage whose address is stable across the call - the sharpest
 * constraint this ABI puts on Phase 4. Finally: call_method's callee
 * resolution IS lookup_property, so the calls area calls this helper rather
 * than declaring a second property read.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::lookup_property (vm/objects.cpp:218) - and Phase 2
 *   should add the prehashed_name overload described under
 *   ct_aot_intern_name, with the std::string one forwarding to it.
 * FAILURE: Full status; *out written ONLY on CT_AOT_OK. The status is
 *   produced by ct_aot_check, not by hand. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_get_prop, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t obj, const struct ct_aot_name *name, struct ct_aot_ic *site, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_get_prop, /* opcode */ get_prop)

/* The write-side IC argument is stronger than usually stated:
 * assign_through_accessor walks up to 64 prototype levels on EVERY named
 * write and returns false for the common plain-data case, and Phaser writes
 * properties in per-frame loops. THE DEVIATION LIST MUST BE COMPLETE,
 * because the two usually omitted are the two that decide whether this row
 * re-enters. store_property has FIVE arms: PROXY is checked FIRST, before
 * is_object(), and calls the trap after allocating string(name) for the
 * argument vector - which is where much of set_prop's allocates=1 comes
 * from; NATIVE lands the write, so `Math.foo = 1` is not dropped;
 * FUNCTION/CLOSURE does find_accessor then call(setter), a SECOND re-entry
 * path independent of assign_through_accessor; `arr.length = n` genuinely
 * RESIZES (spec-capped at 2^32-1, no-op on a typed array, non-
 * finite/negative dropped); and only then is a named write to an array
 * other than length, or a write to a primitive, dropped. A Phase 26 fast
 * path keyed only on is_object() would silently take over the proxy and
 * native arms.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::store_property (vm/objects.cpp), which reaches
 *   context::assign_through_accessor in the same file.
 * FAILURE: Full status. NO out-parameter - writes_a is FALSE and `a` names
 *   the TARGET. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_set_prop, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t obj, const struct ct_aot_name *name, uint64_t v, struct ct_aot_ic *site),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_set_prop, /* opcode */ set_prop)

/* The refusal to merge with get_prop is confirmed by the code: lookup_index
 * tests `target.is_array() && key.is_number()` (with a separate is_view()
 * arm through view_get) and `target.is_string() && key.is_number()` BEFORE
 * it ever reaches lookup_property, so these are two different functions,
 * not one function with a boxed key. Folding them would build a heap string
 * on every named access. Deviations confirmed in objects.cpp: the string
 * path returns string(std::string{text[i]}) - a fresh one-BYTE heap string,
 * not a code point - so `s[0]` allocates with no user code running; an out-
 * of-range index on a view is undefined rather than a throw. The re-entry
 * surprise is the KEY, not the property: the fall-through calls
 * to_string(key), which for an object runs its toString/valueOf.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::lookup_index (vm/objects.cpp) - array+number and
 *   string+number fast paths, else context::to_string of the key then
 *   context::lookup_property.
 * FAILURE: Full status; *out on CT_AOT_OK only. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_get_index, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t obj, uint64_t key, struct ct_aot_ic *site, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_get_index, /* opcode */ get_index)

/* The most important Principle-3 extraction in the area: re-emitting it in
 * ctcompile would be a second implementation of view_set/coerce_element's
 * rules. THE FALL-THROUGH CONDITION IS NOT 'the non-array case': the guard
 * is `target.is_array() && key.is_number()`, so an ARRAY WITH A STRING KEY
 * also falls through to store_property and hits the drop-everything-but-
 * length arm - `a['foo'] = 1` and `a[0] = 1` are different code paths on
 * the same array, and a backend specialising on is_array() alone would
 * miscompile the first. Also note `to_number` here is a MEMBER, not a
 * static free function, and returns NaN for any object without calling
 * valueOf, so `typed[i] = objWithValueOf` stores the coercion of NaN while
 * the identical write to a plain object DOES run toString on the key
 * through the other arm. Neither re-entry nor allocation nor throw is
 * statically decidable, because all three depend on the runtime types of
 * BOTH target and key.
 * DELEGATES TO: TODAY AN INLINE HANDLER BODY (run_loop.cpp, op::set_index,
 *   ~38 lines) that MUST be extracted to context::store_index for both
 *   tiers; the non-fast-path arm falls through to context::store_property
 *   with context::to_string of the key.
 * FAILURE: Full status. No out-parameter - writes_a is FALSE, `a` is the
 *   target. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_set_index, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t obj, uint64_t key, uint64_t v, struct ct_aot_ic *site),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_set_index, /* opcode */ set_index)

/* Verified all-zero, and distinct from set_index for reasons the code
 * states: it is a SILENT NO-OP when the target is not heap_kind::array, it
 * never touches `length`, never consults a prototype, and set_index on the
 * same operands would resize or take the store_property path. One the
 * original omitted: it is unguarded against a VIEW, whose `items` is kept
 * empty while length() reports view_length, so an append onto a typed array
 * pushes into storage nothing reports. Preserve that; do not 'fix' it in
 * emitted code. Carrying `fr` on a mutating helper that does not need it
 * today is deliberate and is a rule for the whole ABI - a generational or
 * incremental collector needs a write barrier, a barrier needs the frame,
 * and adding the parameter later is an ABI break.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::append inline (`if (reg(in.a).is_array())
 *   items.push_back(reg(in.b))`) - extract as context::array_append.
 * FAILURE: None - an all-zero row: no throw edge, no safepoint, no poll.
 *   The compiler may keep every live value in native registers across this
 *   call. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_append, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t array, uint64_t v),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_append, /* opcode */ append)

/* All-zero confirmed: object_object::set grows std::vector and
 * boost::unordered_flat_map, which is malloc and not a heap_object, so
 * nothing here is a GC allocation and no accessor is ever called. THE
 * MISSING ARM: the handler has TWO, and if the source is an ARRAY it copies
 * items[i] under the key std::to_string(i) - `{...[1,2]}` is `{0:1, 1:2}` -
 * so an extraction keeping only the object arm would silently change it.
 * The snapshot-before-loop discipline applies to both arms, because `set`
 * can reallocate the target's storage and target and source may be the same
 * object. Headline deviation to carry in the row: `{...o}` DROPS EVERY
 * ACCESSOR, because accessors live in a separate table this handler never
 * reads - which is also exactly why may_reenter is false where a backend
 * that 'knows JavaScript' would emit a getter call.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::copy_props inline - extract as
 *   context::copy_own_properties.
 * FAILURE: None - all-zero row. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_copy_props, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, uint64_t source),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_copy_props, /* opcode */ copy_props)

/* The void return is not an assumption: compile_delete
 * (expressions.cpp:216-234) emits op::delete_prop and then a SEPARATE
 * emit_const(dst, true), and the non-member case emits false with no opcode
 * at all - so the boolean never comes from this operation and a helper
 * returning bool would be inventing a result the bytecode does not carry.
 * The refusal to merge with delete_index is right and the columns say so:
 * 0/0/0 against 1/1/1. Deviations confirmed: erase() removes a DATA
 * property only (erase_accessor exists and is never called from here), so
 * deleting a getter appears to do nothing; is_object() is heap_kind::object
 * EXACTLY, so delete on an array, proxy, native or closure is a silent no-
 * op; and erase() rebuilds `index` wholesale, which is the invalidation
 * event for any future slot-index IC.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::delete_prop inline (object_object::erase)
 *   - extract as context::delete_property.
 * FAILURE: None - all-zero row. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_delete_prop, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, const struct ct_aot_name *name),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_delete_prop, /* opcode */ delete_prop)

/* Its entire difference from delete_prop is that the key arrives as a value
 * and runs to_string, which for an object runs user JS - and the guard
 * order is exactly as claimed: `if (reg(in.a).is_object())` WRAPS the
 * to_string call, so `delete arr[objKey]`, `delete proxy[objKey]` and
 * `delete "s"[objKey]` never run the key's toString at all, where the
 * spec's ToPropertyKey is unconditional. Hoisting the key coercion out of
 * the guard would start running user code the interpreter does not.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::delete_index inline + context::to_string +
 *   object_object::erase - extract as context::delete_index.
 * FAILURE: Full status. No out-parameter - same separate load-true as
 *   delete_prop. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_delete_index, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, uint64_t key),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_delete_index, /* opcode */ delete_index)

/* Both structural arguments hold. The aliasing point is real -
 * statements.cpp:440 emits `{own_keys, source, source}` and the handler is
 * safe only because make_array runs first and reg(in.a) is written last -
 * and by-value-in/result-out removes the ordering constraint entirely. TWO
 * ADDITIONS. (1) THE ARRAY ARM: an array source yields "0".."n-1" as heap
 * strings and NOT "length", which is why `for (k in arr)` does not see it.
 * (2) SYMBOL FILTERING: each_own_string_key is not each_own_key - it drops
 * every key beginning with the engine's `@@sym:` spelling, and the two
 * walks exist precisely because Object.assign and Reflect.ownKeys keep the
 * unfiltered one, so the extracted member must take the FILTERED walk or
 * for-in starts emitting internal symbol spellings into a page's output.
 * Also: each_own_key interleaves data and accessor keys by insertion
 * position, so definition order is observable and an AOT fast path may not
 * reorder. The unrooted accumulator hazard is this helper's own to fix, not
 * Phase 4's - see the GC note.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::own_keys inline +
 *   object_object::each_own_string_key + context::make_array +
 *   context::string - extract as context::own_string_keys.
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY - returns a well-formed array even after the
 *   ceiling fires; poll ct_aot_failed at back-edges. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_own_keys, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t source), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_own_keys, /* opcode */ own_keys)

/* MERGED: two areas claimed this opcode with two spellings and a third
 * almost let it fall between them. `ct_aot_iterable_values` wins over
 * `ct_aot_iterable` because it matches the delegate's name so a grep finds
 * both ends, and because a bare `ct_aot_iterable` reads as a predicate. It
 * sits with own_keys - for-in and for-of are the same shape - and it is not
 * rare: statements.cpp:445 emits it for EVERY for-of and
 * expressions.cpp:940 for EVERY spread, so a Phaser corpus without it
 * compiles nothing. Cannot be split or open-coded: every branch
 * structurally inspects a heap_object, which constraint 1 forbids. THE
 * ARRAY ARM RETURNS THE SAME ARRAY, not a copy ('Callers must not mutate
 * what they get'), so `for (x of arr)` ALIASES while every other input
 * yields a fresh array; a backend that assumed freshness and hoisted a
 * length read would change behaviour. A GENERATOR IS DRAINED BY RUNNING IT
 * under a 2^20 bound, so an infinite generator HANGS rather than iterating
 * lazily - a deviation to preserve, not fix. `[...proxy]` is silently
 * EMPTY. Both emitters spell it `{iterable, source, source}` with a and b
 * ALIASED, which by-value-in/result-out removes by construction.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::iterable_values (vm/call.cpp:358) - ALREADY A
 *   NAMED MEMBER, no extraction needed. Its six-way dispatch stays put:
 *   is_array passthrough BY IDENTITY (:361), string-by-character, non-
 *   object INCLUDING a proxy to an empty array (:372), __co generator drain
 *   under a 2^20 guard (:384-397), __entries copy (:400), __items copy
 *   (:408), array-like via length + lookup_property under a 2^24 guard
 *   (:416-424).
 * FAILURE: Full status; *out on CT_AOT_OK. THREE CORRECTIONS. (1) The `if
 *   (failed_) break;` swallow is at call.cpp:393 and exists ONLY in the
 *   __co branch - the array-like branch at :416-424 calls lookup_property,
 *   which re-enters accessors and proxy traps and can throw, up to 2^24
 *   times with NO failed_ and NO depth test, so `[...{length: 1e9, get 0(){
 *   throw 0 }}]` runs 16.7 million lookups after the throw was already
 *   caught below. The helper must re-test after EVERY re-entry, not once
 *   after the loop. (2) An unwind is NOT detectable from failed_: when a
 *   generator body throws past its own handlers, generator_resume returns a
 *   normal {value,done:true} record (call.cpp:668), the drain simply breaks
 *   and failed_ is false - the only signal is the frame-depth watermark.
 *   (3) On CT_AOT_FAILED the interpreter still executes `reg(in.a) = ...`
 *   (run_loop.cpp:1112), so *out may be written on a failure path; the
 *   contract is that the caller returns immediately, not that *out is
 *   untouched. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_iterable_values, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t source, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_iterable_values, /* opcode */ iterable)

/* Its only emitter is classes.cpp:95, wiring `extends`. is_object() is
 * heap_kind::object exactly, so it is a silent no-op on anything else - and
 * therefore it CANNOT do what Object.setPrototypeOf does for a closure,
 * which also writes closure_object::proto_link. There is also no cycle
 * check, which is why lookup_property and assign_through_accessor both
 * carry the depth-64 cap; an AOT backend must not assume chains are
 * acyclic.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::set_proto inline
 *   (object_object::prototype) - extract as context::set_prototype.
 * FAILURE: None - all-zero row. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_set_proto, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, uint64_t proto),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_set_proto, /* opcode */ set_proto)

/* Returning the value directly rather than through status/out is right: the
 * row is all zeros, there is no edge to branch on, and the type should say
 * so. Emitted as `{get_proto, dst, dst}` right after load_home
 * (expressions.cpp:598-599), so a and b alias in the interpreter and by-
 * value-in/result-out removes that too. Deviations confirmed line by line:
 * is_object() is heap_kind::object exactly, so this reports undefined for
 * an array, string, proxy, native and CLOSURE (whose chain is
 * closure_object::proto_link, never read here); and a fresh object's
 * prototype defaults to value::null() while classes.cpp:95 sets it only
 * when `extends` is present, so `super.m()` in a BASE-class method reads
 * null. A backend that folded super-dispatch would produce a different
 * program from the interpreter's.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp op::get_proto inline - extract as
 *   context::get_prototype.
 * FAILURE: None - all-zero row. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_get_proto, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target), /* may_throw */ 0,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_get_proto, /* opcode */ get_proto)

/* THE ONE MERGE OF TWO OPCODES INTO ONE HELPER, and it survives the
 * challenge. Verified: the shared handler computes g = getter ? reg(in.c) :
 * undefined and st = getter ? undefined : reg(in.c) and does nothing else
 * with the discriminator; the runtime primitive is already fused as
 * define_accessor(name, getter, setter); and accessor_table::define SKIPS
 * undefined halves, which is exactly what merges a get/set pair into one
 * entry. The inventory's warning at define_setter is about the
 * INTERPRETER's dispatch - the discriminator is the OPCODE ITSELF, read out
 * of in.code - and an AOT site knows statically which it is compiling. The
 * closure arm must stay: a `static get` installs onto the CONSTRUCTOR
 * closure, and target.is_object() alone would drop every static accessor.
 * ONE ASYMMETRY THE EXTRACTION MUST PRESERVE RATHER THAN TIDY:
 * object_object::define_accessor calls erase() first so a shadowing data
 * property is removed, closure_object::define_accessor does NOT, and
 * lookup_property checks closure->find(name) before find_accessor - so a
 * `static get x()` on a class that already has a static data property `x`
 * NEVER RUNS. Harmonising the arms would change observable behaviour and
 * break the Phase 13 differential. may_reenter is false HERE even though
 * every later read or write of that property WILL re-enter; the cost moves
 * to the access site, which is where the barrier and the cache both belong.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp - the SHARED define_getter/define_setter
 *   handler block - plus object_object::define_accessor and
 *   closure_object::define_accessor.
 * FAILURE: None - all-zero row. Installing an accessor only STORES the
 *   function; it never invokes it. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_define_accessor, /* ret */ void,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t target, const struct ct_aot_name *name, uint64_t getter, uint64_t setter),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_define_accessor, /* opcode */ define_getter)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_define_accessor, /* opcode */ define_setter)

/* The four call opcodes share ONE handler body -
 * VM_CASE(call)/(call_method)/(call_computed)/(call_receiver) all fall into
 * run_loop.cpp:927 - and differ only in how callee and receiver were
 * produced, so call_method and call_computed lower as ct_aot_get_prop /
 * ct_aot_get_index followed by this helper, preserving the bytecode's
 * evaluation order without reimplementing a lookup. Routing THROUGH
 * context::call rather than around it gets three things right for free: a
 * generator callee RUNS NOTHING (call.cpp:64), a native callee's
 * current_this_ save/restore (:47-50), and the pending_new_target_ handoff
 * for a non-spread super(), which context::call consumes into
 * entered.new_target (:90-91) and skips on the native and generator paths -
 * exactly op::call_receiver's behaviour. `site` carries the baked
 * diagnostic because callee_origin is a BACKWARDS SCAN of emitted bytecode
 * run at error time against an ip and a register index, neither of which an
 * AOT frame has; ctcompile runs the identical scan at compile time. `key`
 * stays because op::call_computed names its callee with
 * to_string(reg(in.c)) at run time - a SECOND to_string that can re-enter
 * user JavaScript, so dropping it would be a behaviour change, not only a
 * message change. TWO DIVERGENCES THE ORACLE WILL SEE, both because
 * context::call is more careful than op::call: it sizes the window as
 * max(frame_size, argc) and writes EVERY argument (call.cpp:68-73) where
 * op::call reuses the caller's window and guarantees only frame_size+8, and
 * it sets current_this_ for the whole nested loop where op::call sets it
 * only around a native.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::call (CallPaths.def canonical entry,
 *   vm/call.cpp:43) for every accepted callee kind - native (:44-51),
 *   generator (:64 -> make_generator), JS closure (:66-97). The pre-call
 *   acceptance test is op::call's OWN predicate,
 *   is_kind(native)||is_kind(function) (run_loop.cpp:942, 962), which is
 *   NOT the one op::construct uses; only the message ASSEMBLY at
 *   run_loop.cpp:964-991 needs factoring into
 *   context::callee_type_error(form, callee, receiver, site) -
 *   describe_callee (call.cpp:281) and callee_origin (call.cpp:251) are
 *   ALREADY context members declared at vm.hpp:915-917.
 * FAILURE: Full status via ct_aot_check. There is no pending-exception
 *   state in this VM, so the three reachable states after context::call
 *   returns are: OK (depth restored, failed_ clear, *out is the callee's
 *   result); UNWOUND (frames_.size() < the recorded depth, thrown_ already
 *   cleared, and context::call returned value::undefined() -
 *   INDISTINGUISHABLE from a real undefined without the watermark, which is
 *   why ct_aot_enter must record one); FAILED (the 512-deep guard at
 *   call.cpp:77, the ceiling, an UNCAUGHT throw where thrown_ is still set
 *   and must be left alone, or a nested op::halt returning above the
 *   recorded depth). On any non-OK status *out is undefined and must not be
 *   used. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_call, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t callee, uint64_t receiver, const uint64_t *argv, uint32_t argc, uint64_t key, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_call, /* opcode */ call)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_call, /* opcode */ call_method)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_call, /* opcode */ call_computed)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_call, /* opcode */ call_receiver)

/* Cannot fold into ct_aot_call, and the reason is a distinction the
 * bytecode already makes rather than a shape difference: for the same
 * source construct a non-callable is an UNCATCHABLE raise here and a
 * catchable TypeError there, and a callable proxy silently evaluates to
 * undefined here and is a TypeError there. Merging makes the AOT backend
 * pick which of the two the VM meant, which surfaces as a Phase 12A oracle
 * failure rather than a build error. The argument shape reinforces it: the
 * count was not known until the spread was evaluated, so the arguments
 * arrive as an ARRAY VALUE, and unpacking it in AOT code would mean reading
 * array_object::items structurally, which constraint 1 forbids. These two
 * helpers collapse into one the day the VM makes the two failure tiers
 * agree, and not before.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::call, with the argument vector taken from
 *   array_object::items exactly as run_loop.cpp:757-758 does. NO pre-call
 *   acceptance test beyond is_callable().
 * FAILURE: CT_AOT_FAILED when the callee is not callable - op::apply
 *   raise()s 'attempted to call a non-function' (run_loop.cpp:765), the
 *   UNCATCHABLE tier, destination left unwritten. A CALLABLE PROXY yields
 *   CT_AOT_OK with the result undefined: is_callable() admits it
 *   (value.hpp:300-306) and context::call then falls through
 *   !is_kind(function) and returns undefined (call.cpp:52-54) - where the
 *   same source through ct_aot_call is a TypeError. AN ARG_ARRAY THAT IS
 *   NOT AN ARRAY IS NOT AN ERROR: run_loop.cpp:759 tests argv.is_array()
 *   and otherwise leaves the vector EMPTY, so the callee runs with zero
 *   arguments and the status is OK - a backend that asserts or throws there
 *   diverges. Otherwise UNWOUND/FAILED on the same watermark rule. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_call_spread, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t callee, uint64_t arg_array, uint64_t receiver, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_call_spread, /* opcode */ apply)

/* DELEGATING WHOLESALE TO context::construct WOULD BREAK THE VM IN TWO
 * WAYS, which is why the delegate column reads as it does. (a) The
 * acceptance tests DIFFER: op::call rejects a callable proxy
 * (run_loop.cpp:962 accepts only is_kind(function)) while op::construct
 * hands a proxy to context::construct so its construct trap runs
 * (:1421-1428) - sharing one test would break `new Proxy(Renderer2D,
 * {construct(){...}})`, which the code's own comment (call.cpp:430-432)
 * says p5.js executes at its bundle's top level. (b) The ORDER differs:
 * context::construct tests !is_callable() FIRST and raise()s UNCATCHABLY
 * (call.cpp:443-446), whereas op::construct allocates, runs field
 * initialisers and only then throws a CATCHABLE TypeError - delegating
 * wholesale converts a catchable `new` TypeError into an uncatchable engine
 * fault. The pending_new_target_ save/set/restore is what makes this a
 * delegation rather than a reimplementation and removes any prerequisite VM
 * change: op::construct sets fresh.new_target directly, context::call
 * reaches the same field only through pending_new_target_ (already GC root
 * 4), and SAVING AND RESTORING reproduces the second half - op::construct
 * never consumes the flag, so a pass_new_target before a construct leaves
 * it set for whatever pushes a frame next. Reproduce the set AND the leak,
 * or neither. The `constructing` frame flag is deliberately NOT reproduced:
 * its sole reader is run_loop.cpp:1034, and this helper applies
 * is_object_like at the outer level exactly as call.cpp:472-473 does.
 * DELEGATES TO: NOT context::construct wholesale - it must reproduce
 *   op::construct's own dispatch, all of it by delegation: proxy ->
 *   context::construct (call.cpp:428); native -> context::construct;
 *   otherwise context::make_instance (call.cpp:313, byte-identical to the
 *   inline derivation at run_loop.cpp:1447-1455) then
 *   context::run_field_initialisers (call.cpp:477) then, if
 *   !is_kind(function), context::callee_type_error in its `new` form, else
 *   save pending_new_target_, set it to callee, context::call(callee, args,
 *   self), restore, and return is_object_like(produced) ? produced : self.
 * FAILURE: Full status. CAUGHT/UNWOUND for the catchable '`new` on ...'
 *   TypeError of run_loop.cpp:1460 and for anything the field initialisers,
 *   the construct trap or the constructor body throw. CT_AOT_FAILED for the
 *   raise tier, which on the proxy and native paths now includes
 *   context::construct's own non-constructor raise (call.cpp:444). THE
 *   ORDERING IS PRESERVED AND MATTERS: the instance is allocated and
 *   run_field_initialisers has been called BEFORE the is-a-function check,
 *   so an UNWOUND here has already allocated an object with a prototype
 *   installed. run_field_initialisers is a no-op for a non-function
 *   (call.cpp:482), so nothing user-visible runs on that path - only the
 *   allocation is observable, through the collector. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_construct, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t callee, const uint64_t *argv, uint32_t argc, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_construct, /* opcode */ construct)

/* This is the ONE place delegating straight to context::construct is right,
 * and stating that beside ct_aot_construct's correction is the point: the
 * same source construct has two different observable behaviours depending
 * only on whether a spread was written. Keeping them apart stops the AOT
 * backend from deciding which the VM meant. It also inherits, deliberately,
 * the new.target divergence: context::construct reaches the body through
 * call(), which picks up pending_new_target_ - undefined - so `new
 * C(...args)` reports new.target undefined while `new C(args)` reports C.
 * That divergence is the INTERPRETER's, it is reproduced here rather than
 * repaired, and repairing it is a separate change with its own before/after
 * test.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::construct (vm/call.cpp:428) WHOLESALE, with the
 *   argument vector taken from array_object::items as run_loop.cpp:757-758
 *   does, and with NO acceptance test and NO pending_new_target_
 *   manipulation - precisely what op::construct_apply does at
 *   run_loop.cpp:761.
 * FAILURE: CT_AOT_FAILED when the callee is not a constructor -
 *   context::construct raise()s 'attempted to construct a non-function'
 *   (call.cpp:444), the UNCATCHABLE tier, whereas ct_aot_construct's
 *   equivalent is a catchable TypeError. CAUGHT/UNWOUND only from the field
 *   initialisers, the construct trap or the constructor body. A non-array
 *   arg_array yields zero arguments and is not an error. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_construct_spread, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t callee, uint64_t arg_array, const struct ct_aot_site *site, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_construct_spread, /* opcode */ construct_apply)

/* Three things the AOT frame cannot supply structurally, all of which the
 * runtime already has. (1) WHICH PROGRAM the index resolves against is a
 * run-time choice - the enclosing closure's `owner`, not the AOT function's
 * own program - so `new Function` bodies and dynamically imported modules
 * index the right proto table only if the enclosing closure value is passed
 * in; that makes closure_object the first thing Phase 4's frame must carry.
 * (2) An upvalue is either a cell in the ENCLOSING FRAME'S REGISTER or one
 * the enclosing closure already holds, and reading the second would mean
 * walking closure_object::upvalues from AOT code, which constraint 1
 * forbids - so local_upvalues is indexed in parallel with target.upvalues,
 * only the from_parent_local entries are read, and the helper fills the
 * rest from the enclosing closure, substituting undefined when out of range
 * as run_loop.cpp:906-911 does. (3) An arrow's `this` is decided where it
 * is WRITTEN, so the enclosing frame's effective_this is passed and used
 * only when target.is_arrow. function_index is uint32_t because the operand
 * is: `with_bx` splits a 32-bit value across b and c. THE REASON THIS ROW
 * ORIGINALLY GAVE IS FIXED - three of the four op::closure encoders cast the
 * index to uint16_t before building that 32-bit operand, so a program with
 * more than 65,535 protos silently built the wrong closure (measured: 70,001
 * functions, calling 69,999, running 4,463). The casts were deleted on
 * 2026-08-21 and ctcompile/test/ProgramImage.cpp pins the encoding past the
 * old ceiling; this row's uint32_t was right for the operand's own sake all
 * along. WHAT THIS
 * CANNOT DO, and Phase 3 must hear it: the closure_object it returns points
 * at a function_proto in a program, so calling it always re-enters the
 * interpreter - AOT stops at the first nested function unless
 * closure_object or function_proto carries a compiled entry point.
 * DELEGATES TO: a new context::make_closure factored out of
 *   run_loop.cpp:884-920 verbatim - the program selection
 *   (enclosing_closure->owner else *program_), allocate<closure_object>,
 *   `made->owner = &prog`, the upvalue_desc walk, and the is_arrow
 *   captured_this - so run_loop and AOT share one copy rather than two that
 *   can drift.
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY, from three sources rather than one. (1)
 *   allocate()'s ceiling. (2) program_ == nullptr with no enclosing
 *   closure: run_loop.cpp:888-890 dereferences *program_ UNGUARDED, which
 *   is undefined behaviour rather than a fault, and Phase 19's AOT-only
 *   mode is exactly the configuration with no program loaded - the factored
 *   member must guard and raise instead of inheriting the deref. (3)
 *   upvalue_count != target.upvalues.size(), or function_index >=
 *   prog.functions.size(): `prog.functions[in.bx()]` is not bounds-checked
 *   either, and reading local_upvalues past the caller's buffer is worse
 *   than a fault. Poll ct_aot_failed. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_make_closure, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t enclosing_closure, uint32_t function_index, const uint64_t *local_upvalues, uint32_t upvalue_count, uint64_t enclosing_this),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_make_closure, /* opcode */ closure)

/* A TWO-ARGUMENT FORM IS A MISCOMPILE, which is why `constructing` is in
 * the signature. The interpreter's guard is `if (vm_frame->constructing &&
 * !returned.is_object_like())`, and `constructing` is a RUN-TIME property
 * of the frame - set true only by op::construct's inline frame push
 * (run_loop.cpp:1481) and false by every ordinary call including
 * context::construct's own (call.cpp:472 applies the substitution outside
 * instead) - while ONE compiled body serves both `f()` and `new f()`.
 * Applied unconditionally, `function f(){ return 1; }` called as `f()`
 * returns the receiver instead of 1. So it must arrive at run time, which
 * makes the compiled-function entry ABI carry it - a Phase 3 constraint
 * this signature states rather than hides. ret and ret_undef need no helper
 * for the return itself, but lowering ret_undef to `return undefined`
 * miscompiles every `new`, because the constructing-frame substitution is
 * what makes a class constructor with no explicit return evaluate to the
 * instance. Keeping is_object_like behind a helper also stops ctcompile
 * hardcoding a predicate subtler than it looks: an array, a function AND a
 * proxy all count. What this does NOT cover: op::ret also resizes handlers_
 * to handler_base and pops the frame - both belong to ct_aot_leave.
 * DELEGATES TO: value::is_object_like (value.hpp:138-140) - the
 *   substitution at run_loop.cpp:1034-1036, guarded by the frame's
 *   `constructing` flag exactly as the handler guards it.
 * FAILURE: None. It takes no handle, cannot fail, cannot allocate and
 *   cannot re-enter. Declare it nounwind willreturn READONLY - not
 *   readnone: is_object_like calls is_callable, which for a proxy loads
 *   proxy_object::target and recurses (value.hpp:300-306), so it reads
 *   memory, and readnone would license hoisting it above a shadow-frame
 *   pop. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_return_value, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (uint64_t returned, uint64_t receiver, uint32_t constructing),
              /* may_throw */ 0, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_return_value, /* opcode */ ret)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_return_value, /* opcode */ ret_undef)

/* Has REAL tier-1 callers: an async function with no await contains
 * wrap_promise and no await_value, so it is fully AOT-eligible, and the
 * emission is unconditional on every async return path (statements.cpp:223,
 * 604, 770). The test must live inside the helper because it reads an own
 * '__value' off an object_object (constraint 1). THREE facts the lowering
 * must keep: the test is is_object() EXACTLY, so an array, function or
 * proxy returned from an async function is ALWAYS re-wrapped; with no
 * promise_factory_ installed make_promise is the IDENTITY (vm.hpp:692), so
 * AOT must not fold this to 'the result is always an object'; and a PENDING
 * promise is passed through UNWRAPPED, because the pending factory builds
 * it with detail::make_promise and only then flips __settled to false
 * (async.cpp:50-52), so it carries an own __value. Two adjacent handlers
 * therefore use two DIFFERENT shape tests for 'is a promise' - wrap_promise
 * reads __value, is_pending_promise reads __settled (vm.hpp:823-824) - and
 * a backend that unified them would change what `return somePendingPromise`
 * does inside an async function.
 * DELEGATES TO: the already-a-promise test at run_loop.cpp:1148-1149, then
 *   context::make_promise (vm.hpp:691) -> the installed promise_factory_
 *   (builtins/async.cpp:44-45 -> builtins/internal.hpp:339
 *   detail::make_promise).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY (the ceiling), uncatchable, unwinds nothing.
 *   may_reenter=0 verified against the INSTALLED factory rather than the VM
 *   stub: detail::make_promise (internal.hpp:339-347) is new_table,
 *   promise_prototype, four sets and a make_array, with no call() anywhere.
 *   allocate() raises and STILL returns a valid object, so the operation
 *   completes and the result is written; FAILED means 'stop', not 'nothing
 *   happened'. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_wrap_promise, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t v), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_wrap_promise, /* opcode */ wrap_promise)

/* IT DISAGREES WITH THE INVENTORY ROW ON PURPOSE, and the disagreement is
 * verified rather than asserted: await_value's row is (1,1,1,0,1,1,1) but
 * the READ half contains no allocate<> and no call(); raise() only
 * concatenates a std::string built by current_stack (const, allocates no GC
 * object) and unwind_to_handler only resizes vectors. So the row is true of
 * the SUSPEND half only. The split is legitimate ONLY because the DECISION
 * is allocation-free - the interpreter creates vm_frame->async_promise and
 * the coroutine_object strictly AFTER the three-way test at
 * run_loop.cpp:1177-1182 - and that ordering is now an ABI contract, not an
 * observation: if Phase 14 hoists promise creation above the test, this
 * silently becomes a safepoint and every caller's flush disappears. It also
 * preserves two facts: await NEVER re-enters user JS (no then() is called;
 * state is read with own-property find), and is_pending_promise uses
 * is_object() exactly, so a promise behind a Proxy is never seen as
 * pending. Kept even though it has zero tier-1 callers today, because
 * dropping it leaves await_value with no row and lets CT_AOT_SUSPENDED
 * enter the shared enum in Phase 14, making every ctcompile status switch
 * non-exhaustive at once.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::is_pending_promise (vm.hpp:822) plus the read path
 *   at run_loop.cpp:1207-1219 (__rejected -> thrown_ + unwind_to_handler,
 *   else __value). It does NOT contain the suspend path.
 * FAILURE: CT_AOT_OK with *out = the settled __value, or the awaited value
 *   itself when it is not an object, or UNDEFINED when the input is a
 *   pending promise in a context with no
 *   pending_promise_factory_/promise_settler_ - such a promise still
 *   carries __value = undefined (async.cpp:50-52) and run_loop.cpp:1218
 *   takes it, which a headless AOT target must not be surprised by.
 *   CAUGHT/UNWOUND for a settled REJECTED promise. CT_AOT_FAILED when
 *   nothing catches it ('uncaught rejection'). CT_AOT_SUSPENDED when
 *   is_pending_promise(awaited) AND pending_promise_factory_ AND
 *   promise_settler_ are ALL present, exactly as run_loop.cpp:1177 tests
 *   them - and until Phase 14 that status is produced only to be refused by
 *   the caller. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_await_settled, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t awaited, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_await_settled, /* opcode */ await_value)

/* COVERAGE, NOT CAPABILITY. yield_value sits between await_value and
 * wrap_promise in the inventory and 'excluded by the tier-1 rule' is not
 * coverage - Phase 13 checks 93 opcodes against the helper table, so an
 * opcode with no row is a hole whichever way it was reasoned about. This
 * particular hole is where the eligibility predicate sits: function_proto
 * carries is_generator (statements.cpp:751) but the compiler's is_async
 * (compiler_impl.hpp:119) is never copied to the proto and is absent from
 * engine_contract::proto_header (EngineContract.hpp:86-93), so Phase 3's
 * dispatch and ctcompile must each derive the predicate and CANNOT derive
 * it from the same data. Today a disagreement means native code runs off
 * the end of a yield with no diagnostic. This makes it a named engine fault
 * with a stack, costs one call at a site that must be unreachable, and
 * commits to NO suspend ABI - constraint 4 says Phase 14 owns that. Delete
 * it in Phase 14 when a real suspend helper replaces it; the .def edit is
 * one line either way. It covers yield_value ONLY: await_value is owned by
 * ct_aot_await_settled, whose CT_AOT_SUSPENDED outcome is the decision
 * point.
 * DELEGATES TO: context::raise (vm.hpp:932), with the opcode's name from
 *   bytecode_opcodes.def and the JS stack from current_stack()
 *   (vm.hpp:426).
 * FAILURE: Always CT_AOT_FAILED. It produces no value and takes no out-
 *   parameter; the row exists so that 'this opcode has no tier-1 lowering'
 *   is a DECLARED outcome with a diagnosable message rather than an absent
 *   row. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_suspend_unsupported, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint32_t opcode), /* may_throw */ 1,
              /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_suspend_unsupported, /* opcode */ yield_value)

/* What lands in the result is a CELL, not a value - every later read must
 * go through ct_aot_cell_get, because compile/frames.cpp:143-149 marks the
 * local boxed and deliberately emits NO new_cell, so boxing a box would
 * leave the importer reading a cell containing a cell. Both names are
 * compile-time constants and both are genuinely available: the specifier is
 * decode_string_literal(n.text) at frames.cpp:89 and the export name is
 * spec.text/'default' at frames.cpp:136-139, i.e. text the compiler read
 * out of the source. That asymmetry is what justifies string-literal
 * parameters HERE and forbids them for the dynamic-import referrer. The
 * encoding trap - b is the EXPORT name and c the SPECIFIER, the only opcode
 * reading c as a standalone index - disappears by construction with named
 * parameters. Not a safepoint: it reads two flat_maps and, on failure,
 * builds a std::string, which is a C++ heap allocation and not a GC one.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp:1295-1321 inline: current_module_->resolved
 *   lookup (as-written then as-keyed), then modules_ lookup, then
 *   module_record::exports lookup. Nothing new is written.
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY for both misses - 'module X was not loaded'
 *   (:1310) and 'X has no export named Y' (:1316) - uncatchable engine
 *   faults, NOT the ReferenceError a spec-conformant backend would emit.
 *   Returns undefined on those paths; poll ct_aot_failed. The interpreter's
 *   pre-write at :1297 is DEAD on the failure path (raise sets failed_,
 *   VM_NEXT goes straight to vm_done, nothing reads the register), so the
 *   lowering need not reproduce a store the interpreter never observes. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_module_import_cell, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const char *specifier, uint32_t specifier_len, const char *export_name, uint32_t export_name_len),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 0)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_module_import_cell, /* opcode */ load_import)

/* Data flows record -> register, backwards from the name and from
 * bytecode.hpp:201: it ADOPTS the pre-existing cell so a cyclic importer
 * that already took the box keeps a live binding. Cannot merge with
 * ct_aot_module_import_cell: different registry (this module's record vs
 * another's), different failure tier (silent no-op vs uncatchable raise),
 * and it can CREATE the cell where load_import can only find one. A
 * safepoint solely because of that fallback allocation. It needs
 * current_module_, private at vm.hpp:1009 with no accessor - but that is an
 * implementation detail of ctbrowser's own DEFINE expansion, not an ABI
 * question, since the helper is DEFINED in ctbrowser under Principle 11.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp:1270-1292 inline:
 *   current_module_->exports[name], creating a cell_object when absent
 *   (:1287). instantiate_module (call.cpp:110-119) normally created them
 *   all, but browser::instantiate_module is what calls it (browser.cpp:975)
 *   - context::run_module (call.cpp:151-162) does NOT, so a host running a
 *   module without instantiating it first reaches the fallback and the
 *   allocation is live.
 * FAILURE: CT_AOT_OK with *out = the record's cell; CT_AOT_NO_WRITE when
 *   current_module_ is null; CT_AOT_FAILED on the ceiling. THE ONLY HELPER
 *   IN THE TABLE THAT PRODUCES CT_AOT_NO_WRITE, and that is the status's
 *   whole justification: the handler at :1284 simply breaks with no raise
 *   and no thrown_, so execution CONTINUES with the destination register
 *   untouched. writes_a is CONDITIONAL, and if AOT wrote undefined there it
 *   would DESTROY the local being exported, because the register holds that
 *   local on entry (entry.cpp:44-48 passes find_local(local_name) as the
 *   destination). */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_module_export_cell, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const char *name, uint32_t name_len, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_module_export_cell, /* opcode */ bind_export)

/* Kept separate from ct_aot_module_import_cell for a reason stronger than
 * 'the bytecode made the distinction': the two RESULT KINDS have different
 * downstream lowering - a cell every read must funnel through
 * ct_aot_cell_get, versus an ordinary object whose reads are get_prop and
 * therefore accessor RE-ENTRIES, since module_namespace installs a native
 * GETTER per export (call.cpp:137-146). One helper with a nullable name
 * would turn a static property of the lowering into a run-time one. The
 * tempting third option - factoring the shared resolve+lookup into a helper
 * returning module_record* - is UNSAFE: flat_map is
 * boost::unordered_flat_map (core/containers.hpp:30), open-addressing with
 * NO pointer or reference stability, and the installed loader INSERTS into
 * it (browser.cpp:1220-1226), which is why the loader itself re-does find()
 * at :1233 after loading. Any module_record* held across a module-loading
 * helper dangles, so the lookup stays inside each helper. The allocation
 * here is safe under a real collector because the half-built object is
 * stored into of.namespace_object BEFORE the accessor loop
 * (call.cpp:134-135) and that field is GC root 12 - the exact
 * counterexample to ct_aot_iterable_values's unrooted C++ local.
 * DELEGATES TO: the same resolved/modules_ lookup as load_import
 *   (run_loop.cpp:1325-1341), then context::module_namespace (call.cpp:132,
 *   public at vm.hpp:275).
 * FAILURE: RAISE TIER ONLY - 'module X was not loaded' (:1338) or the
 *   ceiling. Same correction as ct_aot_module_import_cell: the :1326 pre-
 *   write of undefined is dead on the raise path and need not be
 *   reproduced. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_module_namespace, /* ret */ uint64_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, const char *specifier, uint32_t specifier_len),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 0, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_module_namespace, /* opcode */ load_namespace)

/* THE REFERRER IS A PARAMETER NO COMPILER CAN FILL, and the tree says so in
 * words: function_proto::module is stamped by the LOADER - `for
 * (script::function_proto & fn : compiled->functions) fn.module =
 * specifier;` at browser.cpp:976, under the comment 'The compiler cannot do
 * this: a specifier is the loader's name for a file.' It is also absent
 * from engine_contract::proto_header, so Phase 15 does not serialize it
 * today. Baking a literal would make the image's referrer the compiler's
 * GUESS at what the loader will call the file, and resolve_specifier
 * (browser.cpp:917-918) rewrites only './' and '../' - so a wrong referrer
 * leaves bare and absolute specifiers working and breaks exactly the
 * relative ones, silently, the worst possible failure distribution. Reading
 * it from fr costs nothing new because the frame must carry the owning
 * proto ANYWAY for current_stack. The heaviest safepoint in the area: TWO
 * user-JS re-entries before it returns (to_primitive_string's
 * lookup_property then call, coerce.cpp:172-177; then a whole module graph
 * through run_reentrant, call.cpp:169-200), and the loader INSERTS into
 * modules_, which is the reference-invalidation hazard above. It may also
 * set suspended_ via the imported module's top-level frame, a flag AOT must
 * not read.
 * DELEGATES TO: run_loop.cpp:1344-1368 inline: context::to_string(spec)
 *   (coerce.cpp:78 -> to_primitive_string at :171 for an object), then the
 *   embedder's module_loader_ (installed at Shell/browser.cpp:1216-1239),
 *   which reaches the VM through run_reentrant. The REFERRER is read by the
 *   helper from fr, not passed in.
 * FAILURE: Full status; *out = the promise on OK. NO LOADER INSTALLED IS
 *   CT_AOT_FAILED, not a no-write: run_loop.cpp:1346 raise()s 'dynamic
 *   import() has no loader installed', which sets failed_, and VM_NEXT then
 *   goes to vm_done - the interpreter does NOT keep going, so a status
 *   meaning 'completed, keep going, do not write' would make AOT code
 *   execute past an uncatchable engine fault. A MISSING MODULE, by
 *   contrast, is CT_AOT_OK carrying an already-REJECTED settled promise
 *   (browser.cpp:1221-1223, 1231-1233) - the lowering must not treat 'the
 *   module was not found' as a control-flow event. */
CT_AOT_HELPER(/* name */ ct_aot_dynamic_import, /* ret */ int32_t,
              /* params */ (struct ct_aot_frame *fr, uint64_t specifier, uint64_t *out),
              /* may_throw */ 1, /* may_reenter */ 1, /* is_safepoint */ 1)
CT_AOT_COVERS(/* helper */ ct_aot_dynamic_import, /* opcode */ dyn_import)

/* THE TEN OPCODES WITH NO HELPER, and why each is correct rather than missing.
 *
 * load_const, load_undef, load_null, load_true, load_false
 *     One-word immediates. The constant pool never holds a heap pointer, so a
 *     compiled image materialises these as literals with no runtime call and no
 *     pool. load_const is a legal RESUME TARGET, so it needs an entry label -
 *     which is not a call.
 * move
 *     A register copy, and the commonest resume target in the instruction set.
 *     Its entry label goes BEFORE the move: on the resume path its source was
 *     filled by the runtime, not by the previous native instruction.
 * jump
 *     Control flow.
 * jump_if_not_nullish, jump_if_defined
 *     Pure tag tests against value's constants, touching no heap. They are
 *     helper-free ONLY while a backend may open-code those constants, which
 *     means value.hpp's qnan_mask, sign_bit, payload_mask and the four tags have
 *     to be pinned the way EngineContract.hpp already pins sizeof(value) == 8.
 *     jump_if_false and jump_if_true are NOT in this list: both call truthy(),
 *     which reads a string's text and a bigint's digits, and a helper is the
 *     only way for compiled code to ask.
 * halt
 *     The function epilogue. Note it does NOT pop a frame the way ret does.
 */

#undef CT_AOT_HELPER
#undef CT_AOT_COVERS
